(window.RK_DATA = window.RK_DATA || {})["pro-en"] = {"modules":[{"id":"a00","title":"Bridge from the basics","sub":"A quick check that everything is in place before Level 2","lessons":[{"id":"a00-1","title":"How Level 2 works","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Welcome to Level 2","md":"You finished the basics. Now we go **deeper**: how traits, iterators, threads, async, macros and `unsafe` really work.\n\nSame rules as before:\n- One lesson, one idea.\n- Short steps. Press \"Next\" at your own pace.\n- \"Deep dive\" cards can be skipped. They are for people who want to know how things work inside.\n\n> **Analogy:** Level 1 taught you to drive. Level 2 opens the hood so you can see the engine."},{"t":"text","title":"What is a bit different","md":"- Examples are longer. Sometimes 20–30 lines. Read them top to bottom, one piece at a time.\n- Sometimes we use **crates** (other people's libraries): `tokio`, `serde`, `rayon`. Rust Playground already has them, nothing to install.\n- More tasks where you write a piece of a real program, not just print a line."},{"t":"text","title":"If it gets hard","md":"That's normal. Level 2 topics are harder. What helps:\n- Go back to the Level 1 lesson on the same topic. The text will point to it.\n- Take a break and do the lesson tomorrow. Your progress is saved.\n- Run the example and change one thing in it. See what changes."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you do with a \"Deep dive\" card if it doesn't interest you right now?","options":["You must read it, or you can't go on","Press \"Next\" and skip it","Start the lesson over"],"answer":1,"why":["No, it is optional.","Right. You can come back to it later.","No need."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Level 2 has the same format, deeper topics.","Crates are already in the Playground.","If it's hard, go back to the basics or take a break."]}],"goals":["See how Level 2 differs from Level 1","Know what to do if it gets hard"]},{"id":"a00-2","title":"Review: ownership and borrowing","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Three ownership rules","md":"1. Every value has an **owner** — one variable.\n2. There can be only **one** owner.\n3. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.\n\nIf you forgot the details, see the Level 1 lesson \"Ownership\"."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"cat\");\n    let b = a; // ownership moved to b\n    println!(\"{b}\");\n}","out":"cat","after":"After `let b = a;` you can't use `a` anymore. Try adding `println!(\"{a}\");` and run it."},{"t":"text","title":"Borrowing","md":"- `&x` — let someone **look**. You can have as many of these as you like.\n- `&mut x` — let someone **change** it. There can be only **one** such reference, and while it exists there are no others.\n\n> **Analogy:** many people can read a library book. But you can only fix it when it's in your hands alone."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many `&mut` references to one value can exist at the same time?","options":["As many as you like","One","Zero, you can't create them"],"answer":1,"why":["That's `&`.","Right. And while it lives, there are no plain `&` either.","You can, if the variable is declared with `mut`."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"To change someone else's value, the function takes `&mut String`, and the call passes `&mut word`.","md":"Fill in the blanks so the function changes the string and `main` prints it.","code":"fn shout(s: ___ String) {\n    s.push('!');\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut word = String::from(\"yay\");\n    shout(___ word);\n    println!(\"{word}\");\n}","answers":[["&mut"],["&mut"]]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `longest` must return the length of the longest string in the list. It must not take the list: after the call, `main` prints it again.","code":"fn longest(words: Vec<String>) -> usize {\n    let mut best = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        if w.len() > best {\n            best = w.len();\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hello\"), String::from(\"Rust\")];\n    println!(\"{}\", longest(words));\n    println!(\"{}\", words.len());\n}","solution":"fn longest(words: &[String]) -> usize {\n    let mut best = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        if w.len() > best {\n            best = w.len();\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hello\"), String::from(\"Rust\")];\n    println!(\"{}\", longest(&words));\n    println!(\"{}\", words.len());\n}","expect":"5\n3","hints":["Let the function take a reference: `words: &[String]`, and call it as `longest(&words)`.","`len()` counts bytes. For English letters that's the same as the number of letters."]},{"t":"recap","items":["One owner. Pass it on and the value moves.","`&` — look (many), `&mut` — change (one).","Functions usually get a reference, not the value itself."]}],"goals":["Recall the three ownership rules","Recall the difference between `&` and `&mut`"]},{"id":"a00-3","title":"Review: enum, Result, traits","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"enum and match","md":"`enum` means \"one of several\". `match` handles every variant.\n\n```\nenum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }\n\nfn action(l: Light) -> &'static str {\n    match l {\n        Light::Red => \"stop\",\n        Light::Yellow => \"wait\",\n        Light::Green => \"go\",\n    }\n}\n```\n`match` must cover **all** variants. Forget one and the compiler reminds you."},{"t":"text","title":"Result and ?","md":"`Result<T, E>` is either `Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`.\n\nA `?` after a call means: \"if it's an error, return it upward right away; if it's a success, take the value out\"."},{"t":"code","code":"fn double(text: &str) -> Result<i32, std::num::ParseIntError> {\n    let n: i32 = text.parse()?;\n    Ok(n * 2)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", double(\"21\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", double(\"cat\").is_err());\n}","out":"Ok(42)\ntrue"},{"t":"text","title":"A trait is a skill","md":"A trait describes **what a type can do**. For example, `Display` means \"I can be printed with `{}`\".\n\n```\ntrait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n```\nAny type can implement a trait with `impl Speak for Type`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `?` after `text.parse()` do?","options":["Prints the error and goes on","On error, leaves the function and returns it; on success, gives the value","Turns the error into 0"],"answer":1,"why":["No, it returns the error from the function.","Right.","That's what `unwrap_or(0)` does."]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement the `Speak` trait for `Cat` and `Dog`. The cat says `Meow`, the dog says `Woof`.","code":"trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.speak());\n    println!(\"{}\", Dog.speak());\n}","solution":"trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Speak for Cat {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"Meow\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Speak for Dog {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"Woof\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.speak());\n    println!(\"{}\", Dog.speak());\n}","expect":"Meow\nWoof","hints":["Start like this: `impl Speak for Cat { fn speak(&self) -> String { ... } }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`enum` + `match` — handle every variant.","`Result` + `?` — errors travel upward by themselves.","A trait is a set of skills; `impl Trait for Type` teaches a type."]}],"goals":["Recall `match` on an `enum`","Recall `Result` and `?`","Recall what a trait is"]},{"id":"a00-p","title":"Practice: warm-up","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Find the first number greater than 10 in the list and print it. If there is none, print `none`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let nums = vec![3, 8, 15, 4, 22];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let nums = vec![3, 8, 15, 4, 22];\n    match nums.iter().find(|&&n| n > 10) {\n        Some(n) => println!(\"{n}\"),\n        None => println!(\"none\"),\n    }\n}","expect":"15","hints":["Iterators have a `find` method. It returns an `Option`.","`nums.iter().find(|&&n| n > 10)` — double `&`, because `find` gives a reference to a reference."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Item` struct holds a name and a price in cents. Add up the cart and print the total in dollars with two digits: `Total: 377.50`.","code":"struct Item {\n    name: String,\n    cents: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cart = vec![\n        Item { name: String::from(\"bread\"), cents: 5500 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"cheese\"), cents: 25000 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"tea\"), cents: 7250 },\n    ];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"struct Item {\n    name: String,\n    cents: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cart = vec![\n        Item { name: String::from(\"bread\"), cents: 5500 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"cheese\"), cents: 25000 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"tea\"), cents: 7250 },\n    ];\n    let total: u32 = cart.iter().map(|i| i.cents).sum();\n    println!(\"Total: {}.{:02}\", total / 100, total % 100);\n}","expect":"Total: 377.50","hints":["`cart.iter().map(|i| i.cents).sum()` gives the sum. Write the type: `let total: u32 = ...`.","Dollars are `total / 100`, cents are `total % 100`. `{:02}` adds a leading zero: `5` → `05`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `parse_age` turns text into an age. An empty string is the error `\"empty\"`, not a number is the error `\"not a number\"`. Use `?` or `match`.","code":"fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"thirty\"));\n}","solution":"fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    if text.is_empty() {\n        return Err(String::from(\"empty\"));\n    }\n    text.parse::<u32>().map_err(|_| String::from(\"not a number\"))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"thirty\"));\n}","expect":"Ok(30)\nErr(\"empty\")\nErr(\"not a number\")","hints":["First check `text.is_empty()` and return `Err(...)`.","`map_err(|_| ...)` swaps one error for another."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`find` looks for the first match and returns an `Option`.","`map` + `sum` give a total in one line.","`map_err` changes the error type."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Warm up before Level 2","Combine structs, `Vec`, `Option` and iterators"]}]},{"id":"a01","title":"Traits, deeper","sub":"Default methods, dyn, associated types, operators","lessons":[{"id":"a01-1","title":"Default methods and traits on top of traits","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Default method","md":"A trait can not only declare a method but also **write its body right away**. Then a type doesn't have to implement it.\n\n```\ntrait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;          // required\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {        // default\n        format!(\"Hello, {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n```\nThe type implements only `name` and gets `hello` for free.\n\n> **Analogy:** a letter template. You fill in the name, the rest of the text is already there."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Hello, {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Robot;\n\nimpl Greet for Cat {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Kitty\") }\n}\n\nimpl Greet for Robot {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"R2\") }\n    fn hello(&self) -> String { String::from(\"BEEP-BOOP\") } // its own\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.hello());\n    println!(\"{}\", Robot.hello());\n}","out":"Hello, Kitty!\nBEEP-BOOP","after":"`Robot` replaced the default method with its own. That's allowed too."},{"t":"quiz","md":"A type implements a trait but doesn't write a method that has a default body. What happens?","options":["Compile error","The type gets the ready-made version","The method will be empty"],"answer":1,"why":["No, that's what the default body is for.","Right.","It does what the trait says."]},{"t":"text","title":"A trait on top of a trait","md":"Sometimes a trait needs another trait. For example: \"to be able to `Report`, a type must be printable with `{}`\".\n\n```\nuse std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Report: Display {\n    fn report(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"[report] {}\", self)\n    }\n}\n```\n`trait Report: Display` reads \"Report requires Display\". Such a \"parent\" trait is called a **supertrait**."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt;\n\ntrait Report: fmt::Display {\n    fn report(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"[report] {}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Temp(i32);\n\nimpl fmt::Display for Temp {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}°C\", self.0)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Report for Temp {}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Temp(21).report());\n}","out":"[report] 21°C","after":"`impl Report for Temp {}` is empty: everything has a default. Remove `impl fmt::Display` and see the error."},{"t":"task","md":"Add a default method `describe` to the `Shape` trait that returns `area: X`, where X is the result of `area()`. The square implements only `area`.","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n    // add describe\n}\n\nstruct Square(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(4).describe());\n}","solution":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"area: {}\", self.area())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Square(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(4).describe());\n}","expect":"area: 16","hints":["A default method can call other trait methods: `self.area()`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"The orphan rule","md":"You can implement a trait for a type only if **the trait or the type is yours** (declared in your crate).\n\nYou can't write `impl Display for Vec<i32>`: both `Display` and `Vec` belong to someone else. Otherwise two crates could do it in different ways, and Rust wouldn't know whose version wins.\n\nThis is the **orphan rule**. The usual way around it is a wrapper: `struct MyList(Vec<i32>);` is your own type, and you can do anything with it."},{"t":"recap","items":["A method with a body in a trait is a default method. It can be overridden.","`trait A: B` — to be `A`, a type must be `B`.","You can't implement someone else's trait for someone else's type."]}],"goals":["Give a trait method a ready-made body","Require one trait for another"]},{"id":"a01-2","title":"Associated types","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A type inside a trait","md":"Remember iterators? The `Iterator` trait has this line:\n\n```\ntrait Iterator {\n    type Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;\n}\n```\n`type Item;` is an **associated type**. Each iterator says **what** it yields: numbers, strings, anything.\n\n> **Analogy:** a vending machine. The trait says \"I can hand out a product\". Which product is up to each machine: one sells chocolate, another sells water."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Container {\n    type Item;\n    fn get(&self, i: usize) -> Option<&Self::Item>;\n    fn first(&self) -> Option<&Self::Item> {\n        self.get(0)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Shelf {\n    books: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Container for Shelf {\n    type Item = String;\n    fn get(&self, i: usize) -> Option<&String> {\n        self.books.get(i)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Shelf { books: vec![String::from(\"Dune\"), String::from(\"The Hobbit\")] };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", s.first());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", s.get(5));\n}","out":"Some(\"Dune\")\nNone","after":"`type Item = String;` — the shelf holds strings."},{"t":"text","title":"Why not a generic?","md":"You could write `trait Container<T>` instead. The difference:\n- `Container<T>` — a type can implement it **many times**: for `T = i32`, for `T = String`…\n- `type Item` — **exactly once**. A shelf holds one kind of thing.\n\nIf the answer is \"one type per implementation\", use an associated type. The code is simpler: no `<T>` everywhere."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many times can one type implement a trait with an associated type `type Item`?","options":["Once","Any number of times, with different `Item`s","Never, such traits exist only in the standard library"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. And it has one `Item`.","That's how a generic trait `Trait<T>` works.","You can write your own."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"The associated type of `Iterator` is called `Item`, and the value is returned inside `Some(...)`.","md":"Fill in the blanks: the counter yields `u32` numbers.","code":"struct Counter { n: u32 }\n\nimpl Iterator for Counter {\n    type ___ = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {\n        if self.n < 3 {\n            self.n += 1;\n            ___(self.n)\n        } else {\n            None\n        }\n    }\n}","answers":[["Item"],["Some"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["`type Item;` in a trait is a type that each implementation chooses.","The implementation writes `type Item = Concrete;`.","One type, one implementation, one `Item`."]}],"goals":["Understand `type Item` inside a trait","Write a trait with your own associated type"]},{"id":"a01-3","title":"impl Trait and dyn Trait","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Problem: different types in one list","md":"A `Vec` holds values of **one** type. What if you want a list of shapes: circles, squares, triangles?\n\n```\nlet shapes = vec![Circle, Square]; // error: the types differ\n```\nThe fix is `Box<dyn Shape>`: \"a box with something that can do Shape\"."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n}\n\nstruct Circle { r: f64 }\nstruct Square { side: f64 }\n\nimpl Shape for Circle {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { 3.14 * self.r * self.r }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"circle\" }\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.side * self.side }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"square\" }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Circle { r: 1.0 }),\n        Box::new(Square { side: 2.0 }),\n    ];\n    for s in &shapes {\n        println!(\"{}: {:.2}\", s.name(), s.area());\n    }\n}","out":"circle: 3.14\nsquare: 4.00"},{"t":"text","title":"Two ways to say \"any Shape\"","md":"- `impl Shape` — \"some **one** concrete type, the compiler knows which\". Fast: the compiler makes a separate copy of the code for each type.\n- `dyn Shape` — \"**any** type, we'll find out while running\". Needed when types are mixed. A bit slower: the method is looked up in a table.\n\n> **Analogy:** `impl` is a pizza order where you know in advance which pizza arrives. `dyn` is a surprise box: open it and see."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\n// impl in an argument: any type with Display fits\nfn show(x: impl Display) {\n    println!(\"<{x}>\");\n}\n\n// impl in a return type: \"I return an iterator, which one doesn't matter\"\nfn evens(max: u32) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n    (0..=max).filter(|n| n % 2 == 0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    show(5);\n    show(\"cat\");\n    let v: Vec<u32> = evens(6).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","out":"<5>\n<cat>\n[0, 2, 4, 6]","after":"`impl Iterator<Item = u32>` as a return type is very common: the real iterator type is long and scary, and this way you don't have to write it."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You need a `Vec` holding both `Circle` and `Square`. What do you pick?","options":["`Vec<impl Shape>`","`Vec<Box<dyn Shape>>`","`Vec<Shape>`"],"answer":1,"why":["`impl` means one concrete type, you can't mix.","Right. `dyn` lets you store different types.","That won't compile: you need `dyn` and a box."]},{"t":"text","title":"Why a box is needed","md":"A `dyn Shape` might be a circle (8 bytes), a square (8 bytes) or something of 100 bytes. Its size is **unknown** in advance, but a `Vec` wants equal slots.\n\n`Box` fixes that: the slot holds only a pointer (known size), and the shape itself lives on the heap. A reference works too: `&dyn Shape`."},{"t":"deep","title":"How dyn works: the method table","md":"A `Box<dyn Shape>` actually holds **two** pointers:\n1. to the data (the circle or square itself);\n2. to a **vtable** — this type's table of methods: \"here is `area`, here is `name`\".\n\nWhen you call `s.area()`, the program looks in the table and jumps to the right function. This is called **dynamic dispatch**.\n\nWith `impl` (and generics `<T: Shape>`) the compiler knows the type in advance and calls the function directly. This is **static dispatch**: faster, but the code is copied for every type.\n\nNot every trait can be used as `dyn`. For example, if a method returns `Self` or is generic itself, the table can't be built. Such traits are not **dyn compatible**."},{"t":"task","mustContain":["dyn Animal"],"md":"Make a list of animals `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>` with a cat and a dog, and print their voices in a loop.","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"meow\") }\n}\n\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"woof\") }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"trait Animal {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"meow\") }\n}\n\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"woof\") }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat), Box::new(Dog)];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.voice());\n    }\n}","expect":"meow\nwoof","hints":["The list type is `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>`. The items are `Box::new(Cat)`, `Box::new(Dog)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`impl Trait` — one concrete type known to the compiler. Fast.","`dyn Trait` — any type, found out while running. Needed for mixed lists.","`dyn` lives behind a pointer: `Box<dyn T>` or `&dyn T`."]}],"goals":["See the difference between `impl Trait` and `dyn Trait`","Put different types into one `Vec`"]},{"id":"a01-4","title":"Operators and From","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Operators are traits too","md":"When you write `a + b`, Rust calls the `add` method of the `std::ops::Add` trait. Implement `Add` for your type and `+` works.\n\n> **Analogy:** the `+` sign is a button. The `Add` trait is the instruction for what happens when you press it."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::ops::Add;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Add for Point {\n    type Output = Point;\n    fn add(self, other: Point) -> Point {\n        Point { x: self.x + other.x, y: self.y + other.y }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", a + b);\n}","out":"Point { x: 11, y: 22 }","after":"See `type Output`? That's an associated type from the last lesson: \"what you get from adding\"."},{"t":"text","title":"Other operators","md":"They all live in `std::ops`:\n- `Sub` — `-`, `Mul` — `*`, `Div` — `/`\n- `Neg` — a minus in front: `-a`\n- `AddAssign` — `+=`\n- `Index` — square brackets: `a[i]`\n\nComparisons `==` and `<` are `PartialEq` and `PartialOrd`. You usually get them with `#[derive]`."},{"t":"text","title":"From and into","md":"`From` is the trait \"how to make my type from another one\".\n\n```\nstruct Meters(f64);\n\nimpl From<f64> for Meters {\n    fn from(x: f64) -> Self { Meters(x) }\n}\n```\nBonus: implement `From<A> for B` and `A` gets an `.into()` method that turns it into `B`."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Celsius(f64);\nstruct Fahrenheit(f64);\n\nimpl From<Celsius> for Fahrenheit {\n    fn from(c: Celsius) -> Self {\n        Fahrenheit(c.0 * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let f = Fahrenheit::from(Celsius(100.0));\n    println!(\"{}\", f.0);\n    let g: Fahrenheit = Celsius(0.0).into();\n    println!(\"{}\", g.0);\n}","out":"212\n32","after":"With `into()` you must say **what** you convert into: `let g: Fahrenheit = ...`. Otherwise the compiler can't tell."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You implemented `impl From<u8> for Level`. What else appeared by itself?","options":["Nothing","An `.into()` method on `u8` that makes a `Level`","`From<Level> for u8`"],"answer":1,"why":["Something useful did.","Right. `Into` comes from `From` for free.","The reverse conversion must be written separately."]},{"t":"order","explain":"First the `use`, then `impl Mul<u32> for Money`, inside it the result type and the method itself.","md":"Put together an implementation of `Mul` for `Money` so that `Money(5) * 3` gives `Money(15)`.","lines":["use std::ops::Mul;","impl Mul<u32> for Money {","    type Output = Money;","    fn mul(self, k: u32) -> Money {","        Money(self.0 * k)","    }","}"]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement `Sub` for `Point` so that `b - a` works.","code":"use std::ops::Sub;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", b - a);\n}","solution":"use std::ops::Sub;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Sub for Point {\n    type Output = Point;\n    fn sub(self, other: Point) -> Point {\n        Point { x: self.x - other.x, y: self.y - other.y }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", b - a);\n}","expect":"Point { x: 9, y: 18 }","hints":["Same as `Add`, only the method is called `sub`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Operators are traits from `std::ops`: `Add`, `Sub`, `Mul`…","`From<A> for B` — how to make a `B` from an `A`. `.into()` appears by itself.","Give `into()` the result type."]}],"goals":["Make your type work with `+`","Convert types with `From` and `into()`"]},{"id":"a01-p","title":"Practice: traits","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Priced` trait has a `price()` method in cents and a default method `with_tax()` that adds 20%. Implement `Priced` for `Book` (price 50000) and print both prices.","code":"trait Priced {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32;\n    fn with_tax(&self) -> u32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Book;\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book;\n    println!(\"{}\", b.price());\n    println!(\"{}\", b.with_tax());\n}","solution":"trait Priced {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32;\n    fn with_tax(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.price() * 120 / 100\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Book;\n\nimpl Priced for Book {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32 { 50000 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book;\n    println!(\"{}\", b.price());\n    println!(\"{}\", b.with_tax());\n}","expect":"50000\n60000","hints":["Adding 20% means multiplying by 120 and dividing by 100."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `total_area` takes a slice of shapes `&[Box<dyn Shape>]` and returns the sum of their areas. Write it.","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n}\n\nstruct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }\nstruct Sq(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.w * self.h }\n}\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn total_area(shapes: &[Box<dyn Shape>]) -> u32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Rect { w: 2, h: 3 }),\n        Box::new(Sq(4)),\n        Box::new(Sq(1)),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", total_area(&shapes));\n}","solution":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n}\n\nstruct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }\nstruct Sq(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.w * self.h }\n}\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn total_area(shapes: &[Box<dyn Shape>]) -> u32 {\n    shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Rect { w: 2, h: 3 }),\n        Box::new(Sq(4)),\n        Box::new(Sq(1)),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", total_area(&shapes));\n}","expect":"23","hints":["`shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `impl From<&str> for Color`: `\"red\"` → `Color::Red`, `\"green\"` → `Color::Green`, anything else → `Color::Unknown`. Then call `.into()`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Color {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Color = \"red\".into();\n    let b: Color = \"green\".into();\n    let c: Color = \"purple\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?} {c:?}\");\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Color {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\nimpl From<&str> for Color {\n    fn from(s: &str) -> Self {\n        match s {\n            \"red\" => Color::Red,\n            \"green\" => Color::Green,\n            _ => Color::Unknown,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Color = \"red\".into();\n    let b: Color = \"green\".into();\n    let c: Color = \"purple\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?} {c:?}\");\n}","expect":"Red Green Unknown","hints":["Inside `from`, write `match s { \"red\" => ..., \"green\" => ..., _ => ... }`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Describe` trait requires `Debug` (a supertrait). Its default method `describe` returns `This is: ` and the debug print of the value. Finish the trait and implement it for `Pet`.","code":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"This is: {:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pet {\n    name: String,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pet { name: String::from(\"Bagel\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", p.describe());\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\ntrait Describe: Debug {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"This is: {:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pet {\n    name: String,\n}\n\nimpl Describe for Pet {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pet { name: String::from(\"Bagel\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", p.describe());\n}","expect":"This is: Pet { name: \"Bagel\" }","hints":["Right now the trait doesn't know that `self` can be printed with `{:?}`. Write `trait Describe: Debug`.","The implementation can be empty: `impl Describe for Pet {}`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Default methods save code.","`dyn` puts different types into one list.","`From` does conversions, and `.into()` comes as a bonus."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Practice default methods, `dyn` and `From`"]}]},{"id":"a02","title":"Iterators from the inside","sub":"Your own iterator, IntoIterator, fold, collect into anything","lessons":[{"id":"a02-1","title":"Your own iterator","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An iterator is just next","md":"To become an iterator, a type implements **one** method:\n\n```\nfn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>\n```\n- More values left — return `Some(value)`.\n- Out of values — return `None`.\n\nEverything else (`map`, `filter`, `sum`, `collect`…) is a default method. They appear by themselves.\n\n> **Analogy:** a napkin dispenser. Pull and you get a napkin. Empty, and nothing comes out."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Countdown {\n    n: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.n == 0 {\n            None\n        } else {\n            self.n -= 1;\n            Some(self.n + 1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for x in (Countdown { n: 3 }) {\n        println!(\"{x}\");\n    }\n    println!(\"liftoff!\");\n}","out":"3\n2\n1\nliftoff!","after":"Why `&mut self`? The iterator **remembers where it stopped** and updates that state on every step."},{"t":"code","md":"Your own iterator can already do everything normal ones can:","code":"struct Countdown {\n    n: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.n == 0 {\n            None\n        } else {\n            self.n -= 1;\n            Some(self.n + 1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let total: u32 = Countdown { n: 5 }.filter(|x| x % 2 == 1).sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","out":"9","after":"5 + 3 + 1 = 9. We wrote only `next`, and `filter` and `sum` came for free."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which method must you implement for a type to become an iterator?","options":["`iter`","`next`","`map` and `filter`"],"answer":1,"why":["That's a collection method, not an iterator one.","Right. Everything else is a default.","They are already in the trait."]},{"t":"text","title":"Endless iterators","md":"`next` may **never** return `None`. That's fine — just take as many as you need with `take`.\n\n```\nstruct Fib { a: u64, b: u64 }\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        let r = self.a;\n        self.a = self.b;\n        self.b += r;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n```\n`Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(10)` gives the first 10 Fibonacci numbers."},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the `Fib` iterator and print the first 8 Fibonacci numbers on one line by collecting into a `Vec`.","code":"struct Fib {\n    a: u64,\n    b: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u64> = Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(8).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Fib {\n    a: u64,\n    b: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        let r = self.a;\n        self.a = self.b;\n        self.b += r;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u64> = Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(8).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13]","hints":["Remember the current `a`, then shift: `a` becomes `b`, and `b` becomes the sum of the old ones.","`let r = self.a; self.a = self.b; self.b += r; Some(r)`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Where the state lives","md":"Every iterator has its own state: a counter, a position in an array, the last two Fibonacci numbers.\n\nWhen you write `v.iter().map(f).filter(g)`, you get a **nesting doll** of structs: `Filter<Map<Iter<i32>, F>, G>`. Each one holds the inner one and its own closure. Calling `next` on the outer one calls `next` on the inner one.\n\nThe compiler can flatten this doll into a simple loop. That's why an iterator chain in Rust is usually as fast as a hand-written `for` loop. This is called a **zero-cost abstraction**."},{"t":"recap","items":["Iterator = `type Item` + `fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Item>`.","`None` means the end. You may never end and use `take(n)`.","All adapters (`map`, `filter`, `sum`…) come for free."]}],"goals":["Write a type that can do `next()`","Get every iterator method for free"]},{"id":"a02-2","title":"IntoIterator: how for works","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What for really does","md":"The line `for x in collection` turns into roughly this:\n\n```\nlet mut it = collection.into_iter();\nwhile let Some(x) = it.next() {\n    // loop body\n}\n```\n`into_iter()` is a method of the `IntoIterator` trait: \"how to make an iterator out of me\"."},{"t":"text","title":"Three ways to walk a Vec","md":"- `for x in v.iter()` (or `for x in &v`) — `x` is `&T`. We look.\n- `for x in v.iter_mut()` (or `for x in &mut v`) — `x` is `&mut T`. We change.\n- `for x in v` (or `v.into_iter()`) — `x` is `T`. We take. After the loop `v` is gone.\n\n> **Analogy:** a box of candy. Look at each one, rearrange each one, or eat them all. After the third, the box is empty."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    for x in &mut v {\n        *x *= 10;\n    }\n    for x in &v {\n        print!(\"{x} \");\n    }\n    println!();\n    let total: i32 = v.into_iter().sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","out":"10 20 30\n60","after":"`*x *= 10` — the star is needed because `x` is a reference here, and we change the number itself."},{"t":"quiz","md":"After `for s in names { ... }` where `names: Vec<String>` — can you still use `names`?","options":["Yes, the loop changes nothing","No, a by-value `for` took the vector","Yes, but only `names.len()`"],"answer":1,"why":["The loop took ownership.","Right. Write `for s in &names` if you still need the vector.","Not even that."]},{"t":"text","title":"Your own type in for","md":"Want to write `for x in &my_list`? Implement `IntoIterator` for `&MyList`. The easiest way is to hand out the inner vector's iterator:\n\n```\nstruct Playlist { songs: Vec<String> }\n\nimpl<'a> IntoIterator for &'a Playlist {\n    type Item = &'a String;\n    type IntoIter = std::slice::Iter<'a, String>;\n    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {\n        self.songs.iter()\n    }\n}\n```\n`'a` is a lifetime: the items live as long as the playlist. More in the lifetimes module."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Playlist {\n    songs: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> IntoIterator for &'a Playlist {\n    type Item = &'a String;\n    type IntoIter = std::slice::Iter<'a, String>;\n    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {\n        self.songs.iter()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Playlist { songs: vec![String::from(\"Intro\"), String::from(\"Outro\")] };\n    for s in &p {\n        println!(\"♪ {s}\");\n    }\n    println!(\"total: {}\", p.songs.len());\n}","out":"♪ Intro\n♪ Outro\ntotal: 2"},{"t":"fill","explain":"`iter_mut()` gives `&mut i32`, and `*x` is the number itself that we change.","md":"Fill in the blanks: double each number in place, then print the vector.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    for x in v.___() {\n        ___x *= 2;\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","answers":[["iter_mut"],["*"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["`for x in c` = `c.into_iter()` + `next()` in a loop.","`&v` → `&T`, `&mut v` → `&mut T`, `v` → `T` (the vector is eaten).","Your type in `for` — implement `IntoIterator`."]}],"goals":["See that `for` calls `into_iter()`","Tell apart `iter()`, `iter_mut()`, `into_iter()`"]},{"id":"a02-3","title":"Adapters people forget","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Pair up and join","md":"- `a.zip(b)` — walk two iterators **in pairs**: `(a1, b1)`, `(a2, b2)`… It ends when the shorter one ends.\n- `a.chain(b)` — all of `a`, then all of `b`.\n- `enumerate()` — add a number: `(0, x)`, `(1, y)`…"},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\"];\n    let scores = [90, 75, 82];\n    for (n, s) in names.iter().zip(scores.iter()) {\n        println!(\"{n}: {s}\");\n    }\n    let all: Vec<i32> = (1..3).chain(7..9).collect();\n    println!(\"{all:?}\");\n}","out":"Ann: 90\nBob: 75\nVicky: 82\n[1, 2, 7, 8]"},{"t":"text","title":"Windows and chunks (on slices)","md":"- `v.windows(2)` — a sliding window: `[a,b]`, `[b,c]`, `[c,d]`…\n- `v.chunks(2)` — slicing without overlap: `[a,b]`, `[c,d]`, `[e]`.\n\n> **Analogy:** `windows` is looking out of a train window: each frame is a bit shifted. `chunks` is slicing a loaf of bread."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let temps = [3, 5, 4, 8, 10];\n    let rises = temps.windows(2).filter(|w| w[1] > w[0]).count();\n    println!(\"it got warmer {rises} times\");\n\n    for part in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunks(2) {\n        println!(\"{part:?}\");\n    }\n}","out":"it got warmer 3 times\n[1, 2]\n[3, 4]\n[5]"},{"t":"text","title":"Stop and skip","md":"- `take_while(condition)` — take **while** the condition is true. First \"false\" — stop.\n- `skip_while(condition)` — skip while true, then take everything.\n- `step_by(n)` — every n-th one.\n- `rev()` — backwards."},{"t":"text","title":"Unfold nested things","md":"`flat_map` is like `map`, but each item turns into **several**, and everything is glued into one stream.\n\n```\nlet words = [\"cat\", \"dog\"];\nlet letters: Vec<char> = words.iter().flat_map(|w| w.chars()).collect();\n// ['c', 'a', 't', 'd', 'o', 'g']\n```\nIf the items are already iterators or `Vec`s, `flatten()` is enough."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `[1, 2, 3, 10, 4].iter().take_while(|&&x| x < 5).count()` return?","options":["4","3","5"],"answer":1,"why":["`take_while` stops at the first non-match, and the `4` after `10` isn't counted.","Right. `1, 2, 3`, then `10` — stop.","Not all items are below 5."]},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many times two neighboring numbers in the list are **equal**. Use `windows(2)`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let v = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let v = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4];\n    let pairs = v.windows(2).filter(|w| w[0] == w[1]).count();\n    println!(\"{pairs}\");\n}","expect":"3","hints":["`v.windows(2)` gives slices of two items: `w[0]` and `w[1]`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`zip` — in pairs, `chain` — one after another, `enumerate` — with a number.","`windows` — a sliding window, `chunks` — pieces.","`take_while`, `skip_while`, `step_by`, `rev` — steer the stream.","`flat_map`/`flatten` — unfold nested things."]}],"goals":["Meet `zip`, `chain`, `windows`, `flat_map`, `take_while`","Build short pipelines instead of loops"]},{"id":"a02-4","title":"fold and collect into anything","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"fold — roll everything into one","md":"`fold(start, |accumulated, x| new_accumulated)`.\n\n```\nlet sum = [1, 2, 3].iter().fold(0, |acc, x| acc + x); // 6\n```\n`sum`, `count`, `max` are special cases of `fold`. When there's no ready-made method, use `fold`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a snowball. Roll it through the snow and with every step it picks up a bit more."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let words = [\"rust\", \"is\", \"delightful\"];\n    let longest = words.iter().fold(\"\", |best, &w| {\n        if w.chars().count() > best.chars().count() { w } else { best }\n    });\n    println!(\"{longest}\");\n\n    let sentence = words.iter().fold(String::new(), |mut s, w| {\n        if !s.is_empty() { s.push(' '); }\n        s.push_str(w);\n        s\n    });\n    println!(\"{sentence}\");\n}","out":"delightful\nrust is delightful"},{"t":"text","title":"collect can do a lot","md":"`collect()` looks at the **type** you ask for and builds it:\n- `Vec<T>` — a list.\n- `String` — from chars or strings.\n- `HashMap<K, V>` or `BTreeMap<K, V>` — from `(key, value)` pairs.\n- `Result<Vec<T>, E>` — from a stream of `Result`s. The first error makes the whole result `Err`."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let s: String = ['R', 'u', 's', 't'].iter().collect();\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n\n    let ages: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = [(\"Ann\", 30), (\"Bob\", 25)].into_iter().collect();\n    println!(\"{ages:?}\");\n\n    let good: Result<Vec<i32>, _> = [\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"].iter().map(|x| x.parse::<i32>()).collect();\n    println!(\"{good:?}\");\n\n    let bad: Result<Vec<i32>, _> = [\"1\", \"cat\", \"3\"].iter().map(|x| x.parse::<i32>()).collect();\n    println!(\"{}\", bad.is_err());\n}","out":"Rust\n{\"Ann\": 30, \"Bob\": 25}\nOk([1, 2, 3])\ntrue","after":"`BTreeMap` keeps keys in order. So the output is always the same — handy for examples."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you get if you collect `[\"4\", \"x\", \"6\"]` into `Result<Vec<i32>, _>` with `parse`?","options":["`Ok([4, 6])`","`Err(...)`","`Ok([4, 0, 6])`"],"answer":1,"why":["Errors aren't silently dropped.","Right. One error and the whole result is an error.","Rust doesn't put in a zero by itself."]},{"t":"text","title":"peekable — peek at the next one","md":"Sometimes you need to look at the next item **without taking** it. That's `peekable()`:\n\n```\nlet mut it = [1, 2, 3].iter().peekable();\nif let Some(&&next) = it.peek() {\n    // next == 1, but it's still in the iterator\n}\n```\nUseful when parsing text: \"is the next char a digit? then keep reading the number\"."},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many times each word appears. Collect into a `BTreeMap<&str, u32>` and print it.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{counts:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        *counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"{counts:?}\");\n}","expect":"{\"cat\": 3, \"dog\": 2, \"fish\": 1}","hints":["`text.split_whitespace()` gives the words.","`*counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;` — bump the word's counter, creating it the first time."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`fold(start, |acc, x| ...)` — roll into one value.","`collect` builds the type you asked for.","`Result<Vec<_>, _>` via `collect` — stop at the first error.","`peekable` — look at the next one without taking it."]}],"goals":["Fold a stream into one value with `fold`","Collect into `String`, `HashMap`, `Result<Vec>`"]},{"id":"a02-p","title":"Practice: iterators","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Steps` iterator yields numbers from `start` to `end` inclusive with a `step`. Finish `next`.","code":"struct Steps {\n    cur: u32,\n    end: u32,\n    step: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Steps {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u32> = Steps { cur: 2, end: 11, step: 3 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Steps {\n    cur: u32,\n    end: u32,\n    step: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Steps {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.cur > self.end {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let r = self.cur;\n        self.cur += self.step;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u32> = Steps { cur: 2, end: 11, step: 3 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[2, 5, 8, 11]","hints":["If `cur` is already past `end` — `None`. Otherwise remember `cur`, add `step` and return the remembered value."]},{"t":"task","md":"You have names and scores. Print only those above 80, as `name — score`. Use `zip` and `filter`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\", \"Greg\"];\n    let scores = [95, 70, 88, 80];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\", \"Greg\"];\n    let scores = [95, 70, 88, 80];\n    for (n, s) in names.iter().zip(scores.iter()).filter(|(_, s)| **s > 80) {\n        println!(\"{n} — {s}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"Ann — 95\nVicky — 88","hints":["`names.iter().zip(scores.iter())` gives pairs `(&name, &score)`.","`filter` gets a reference to the pair, so `**s > 80`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Turn the string `\"3,8,x,1\"` into the sum of its numbers, **skipping** whatever isn't a number. Use `filter_map`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let line = \"3,8,x,1\";\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let line = \"3,8,x,1\";\n    let sum: i32 = line.split(',').filter_map(|p| p.parse::<i32>().ok()).sum();\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"12","hints":["`filter_map` keeps only the `Some`s. And `.ok()` turns a `Result` into an `Option`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Use `fold` to find the minimum and the maximum of the list at the same time and print `min 2, max 9`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let v = [5, 2, 9, 4];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let v = [5, 2, 9, 4];\n    let (lo, hi) = v.iter().fold((i32::MAX, i32::MIN), |(lo, hi), &x| (lo.min(x), hi.max(x)));\n    println!(\"min {lo}, max {hi}\");\n}","expect":"min 2, max 9","hints":["The accumulated value can be a pair: `(minimum, maximum)`.","Start with `(i32::MAX, i32::MIN)`, and on each step `(lo.min(x), hi.max(x))`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Your own iterator — just `next`.","`zip` + `filter`, `filter_map` + `ok()`, `fold` with a pair are common tricks."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write your own iterator and a couple of pipelines"]}]},{"id":"a03","title":"Closures, deeper","sub":"Fn, FnMut, FnOnce, move, and functions that return functions","lessons":[{"id":"a03-1","title":"Three kinds of closures","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A closure takes something from around it","md":"A closure can use variables from outside. **How** it uses them decides its kind:\n\n- `Fn` — only **looks**. You can call it as often as you like.\n- `FnMut` — **changes** things. Can also be called many times, but the closure itself must be `mut`.\n- `FnOnce` — **takes** things for itself (eats them). It can be called only once.\n\n> **Analogy:** a guest in your home. `Fn` looks at the paintings. `FnMut` moves the furniture around. `FnOnce` takes the cake home. You can't take the cake twice."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ann\");\n\n    // Fn: only reads name\n    let greet = || println!(\"Hi, {name}\");\n    greet();\n    greet();\n\n    // FnMut: changes count\n    let mut count = 0;\n    let mut tick = || count += 1;\n    tick();\n    tick();\n    println!(\"ticks: {count}\");\n\n    // FnOnce: takes name and hands it out\n    let take = || name;\n    let got = take();\n    println!(\"taken: {got}\");\n}","out":"Hi, Ann\nHi, Ann\nticks: 2\ntaken: Ann","after":"Try calling `take()` a second time. The compiler will say the closure has already been \"eaten\"."},{"t":"text","title":"They are also requirements","md":"`Fn`, `FnMut`, `FnOnce` are traits. A function can say **which** closure it accepts:\n\n```\nfn twice<F: Fn()>(f: F) { f(); f(); }\nfn twice_mut<F: FnMut()>(mut f: F) { f(); f(); }\nfn once<F: FnOnce()>(f: F) { f(); }\n```\n- `FnOnce` is the loosest requirement: **any** closure fits.\n- `Fn` is the strictest: only a \"looking\" closure fits."},{"t":"quiz","md":"The closure `|| v.push(1)`, where `v` is a vector outside. Which kind is it?","options":["`Fn`","`FnMut`","`FnOnce`"],"answer":1,"why":["`push` changes the vector, looking is not enough.","Right. It changes `v`.","It doesn't take `v`, it only changes it."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"A function asks for `F: FnOnce()`. Can you pass it an `Fn` closure?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. Something that can be called many times can be called once.","`FnOnce` is the loosest requirement, anything fits."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `apply_n` must call the closure `n` times. Pick the right requirement so the counter works.","code":"fn apply_n<F: Fn()>(n: u32, f: F) {\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        f();\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    apply_n(4, || total += 5);\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","solution":"fn apply_n<F: FnMut()>(n: u32, mut f: F) {\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        f();\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    apply_n(4, || total += 5);\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"20","hints":["The closure changes `total`. So it's `FnMut`.","Don't forget `mut f: F` — an `FnMut` can only be called through a mutable variable."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How the compiler picks the kind","md":"The compiler looks at what the closure does with each captured variable and picks the **gentlest** way to capture it:\n- only reads — captures by `&`;\n- changes — by `&mut`;\n- hands it out or passes it to someone by value — takes it.\n\nThe resulting closure implements several traits at once. Every `Fn` is also an `FnMut`, and every `FnMut` is also an `FnOnce`. Like nesting dolls: `Fn` ⊂ `FnMut` ⊂ `FnOnce`.\n\nUnder the hood a closure is a nameless struct. Its fields are the captured variables (or references to them). And calling it is a method on that struct."},{"t":"recap","items":["`Fn` looks, `FnMut` changes, `FnOnce` takes.","As a requirement: `FnOnce` accepts everything, `Fn` is the strictest.","Call an `FnMut` through a `mut` variable."]}],"goals":["See how `Fn`, `FnMut` and `FnOnce` differ","Guess which one you'll get"]},{"id":"a03-2","title":"move: take it with you","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What move does","md":"Normally a closure **borrows** outside variables. `move` makes it **take** them (move them inside).\n\n```\nlet name = String::from(\"Bob\");\nlet f = move || println!(\"{name}\");\n// name is gone here: it's inside f\n```\nNote: `move` changes how things are **captured**, but doesn't necessarily make the closure `FnOnce`. This `f` still only reads `name`, so it's `Fn`, and you can call it many times."},{"t":"text","title":"Where move is needed","md":"When the closure **lives longer** than the variables around it:\n- it's handed to another thread (`thread::spawn`);\n- it's returned from a function;\n- it's put into a struct that will travel far.\n\nWithout `move`, the closure would hold references to variables that are about to disappear. Rust won't allow that.\n\n> **Analogy:** leaving home for a long time, you take your things with you, not a note saying \"umbrella's in the closet\"."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {\n        let sum: i32 = data.iter().sum();\n        println!(\"sum in the thread: {sum}\");\n    });\n    handle.join().unwrap();\n}","out":"sum in the thread: 6","after":"Remove `move` and run it. The compiler will say the thread may outlive `data`."},{"t":"text","title":"move and copyable types","md":"If the captured variable is `Copy` (numbers, `bool`, `char`), `move` simply puts a **copy** inside. The outside variable stays.\n\n```\nlet x = 5;\nlet f = move || x + 1;\nprintln!(\"{x} {}\", f()); // 5 6 — x is alive\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Do you need `move` in `thread::spawn(|| println!(\"{v:?}\"))`, where `v` is a vector from `main`?","options":["Yes","No, `println!` doesn't change anything","Only if `v` is empty"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. The thread may live longer than `main`, so it needs its own data.","It's not about changing, it's about lifetime.","Whether the vector is empty doesn't matter."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`move` — take the captured things into the closure.","Needed when the closure outlives its surroundings: threads, returning from a function.","`Copy` values are copied by `move`."]}],"goals":["See why you write `move` before a closure","Know where `move` is required"]},{"id":"a03-3","title":"Return a closure","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function factory","md":"A function can **create** and return a closure:\n\n```\nfn make_adder(n: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x + n\n}\n```\n`make_adder(5)` is a new function \"add 5\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a rubber stamp. You set it once (\"5\"), then stamp as often as you like."},{"t":"code","code":"fn make_adder(n: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x + n\n}\n\nfn make_greeter(greeting: &str) -> impl Fn(&str) -> String {\n    let g = greeting.to_string();\n    move |name| format!(\"{g}, {name}!\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let add5 = make_adder(5);\n    let add10 = make_adder(10);\n    println!(\"{} {}\", add5(1), add10(1));\n\n    let hi = make_greeter(\"Hello\");\n    println!(\"{}\", hi(\"Vicky\"));\n}","out":"6 11\nHello, Vicky!","after":"`move` is required here: `n` and `g` die when the function ends, but the closure keeps living."},{"t":"text","title":"When you need Box<dyn Fn>","md":"`impl Fn` means \"one concrete type\". What if a function returns **different** closures in different branches?\n\n```\nfn op(name: &str) -> Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32> {\n    match name {\n        \"+\" => Box::new(|a, b| a + b),\n        \"*\" => Box::new(|a, b| a * b),\n        _ => Box::new(|_, _| 0),\n    }\n}\n```\nEach closure has its own type, even if they look alike. So — a box with `dyn`, like in the traits module."},{"t":"code","code":"fn op(name: &str) -> Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32> {\n    match name {\n        \"+\" => Box::new(|a, b| a + b),\n        \"*\" => Box::new(|a, b| a * b),\n        _ => Box::new(|_, _| 0),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for name in [\"+\", \"*\", \"?\"] {\n        let f = op(name);\n        println!(\"6 {name} 7 = {}\", f(6, 7));\n    }\n}","out":"6 + 7 = 13\n6 * 7 = 42\n6 ? 7 = 0"},{"t":"quiz","md":"A function returns `|x| x + 1` in one `if` branch and `|x| x * 2` in the other. What goes in the return type?","options":["`impl Fn(i32) -> i32`","`Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>`","`Fn(i32) -> i32`"],"answer":1,"why":["These are two different types, `impl` can't unite them.","Right.","You can't write it without `impl` or `dyn`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Plain functions fit too","md":"The type `fn(i32) -> i32` (lowercase `f`) is a **function pointer**. It's a plain function, or a closure that captures nothing.\n\n```\nfn double(x: i32) -> i32 { x * 2 }\nlet f: fn(i32) -> i32 = double;\nlet v: Vec<i32> = [1, 2].iter().map(|&x| f(x)).collect();\n```\nA plain function can go anywhere an `Fn` is expected: `.map(double)` works too."},{"t":"task","md":"Write `make_multiplier(k)`, which returns a closure \"multiply by k\".","code":"fn make_multiplier(k: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let triple = make_multiplier(3);\n    println!(\"{}\", triple(7));\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3].into_iter().map(make_multiplier(10)).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn make_multiplier(k: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x * k\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let triple = make_multiplier(3);\n    println!(\"{}\", triple(7));\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3].into_iter().map(make_multiplier(10)).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"21\n[10, 20, 30]","hints":["The body is one line: `move |x| x * k`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`-> impl Fn(A) -> B` — return one closure. Almost always with `move`.","Different closures in different branches — `Box<dyn Fn(A) -> B>`.","`fn(A) -> B` — a pointer to a plain function."]}],"goals":["Write a function that returns a function","Choose between `impl Fn` and `Box<dyn Fn>`"]},{"id":"a03-4","title":"Closures in structs","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function field","md":"You can put a closure into a struct. Two ways:\n\n```\nstruct Button<F: Fn()> {\n    on_click: F,                 // one concrete type, fast\n}\n\nstruct Menu {\n    actions: Vec<Box<dyn Fn()>>, // many different closures\n}\n```\nThe second way is for when there are many handlers and they differ."},{"t":"code","code":"struct EventBus {\n    handlers: Vec<Box<dyn Fn(&str)>>,\n}\n\nimpl EventBus {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        EventBus { handlers: Vec::new() }\n    }\n    fn subscribe(&mut self, f: impl Fn(&str) + 'static) {\n        self.handlers.push(Box::new(f));\n    }\n    fn emit(&self, event: &str) {\n        for h in &self.handlers {\n            h(event);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bus = EventBus::new();\n    bus.subscribe(|e| println!(\"log: {e}\"));\n    let prefix = String::from(\">>\");\n    bus.subscribe(move |e| println!(\"{prefix} {}\", e.to_uppercase()));\n    bus.emit(\"save\");\n}","out":"log: save\n>> SAVE","after":"`+ 'static` means \"the closure holds no references to temporary things\". That's why `prefix` needs `move`."},{"t":"text","title":"Calling a field","md":"If a closure sits in a field, the call goes in parentheses:\n\n```\n(self.on_click)();\n```\nWithout them, `self.on_click()` makes Rust look for a **method** `on_click`, not the field."},{"t":"fill","explain":"`(self.on_click)()` — first get the field, then call it.","md":"Fill in the blanks so the button calls its handler.","code":"struct Button<F: Fn()> {\n    on_click: F,\n}\n\nimpl<F: Fn()> Button<F> {\n    fn press(&self) {\n        ___self.on_click___();\n    }\n}","answers":[["("],[")"]]},{"t":"task","md":"Make a `Counter` that stores its step as a closure `Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>` and applies it in the `next` method. Start at 1, the step multiplies by 3. Print three values.","code":"struct Counter {\n    value: i32,\n    step: Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>,\n}\n\nimpl Counter {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> i32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Counter { value: 1, step: Box::new(|x| x * 3) };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n}","solution":"struct Counter {\n    value: i32,\n    step: Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>,\n}\n\nimpl Counter {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> i32 {\n        self.value = (self.step)(self.value);\n        self.value\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Counter { value: 1, step: Box::new(|x| x * 3) };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n}","expect":"3\n9\n27","hints":["Calling a field goes in parentheses: `(self.step)(self.value)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A closure in a field: `F: Fn()` (one type) or `Box<dyn Fn()>` (different ones).","Call a field: `(self.f)(args)`.","`+ 'static` — the closure holds no temporary references."]}],"goals":["Store a closure in a struct field","Build a simple event handler system"]},{"id":"a03-p","title":"Practice: closures","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The function `compose(f, g)` returns a new function \"first `f`, then `g`\". Finish it.","code":"fn compose(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32, g: impl Fn(i32) -> i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let inc_then_double = compose(|x| x + 1, |x| x * 2);\n    println!(\"{}\", inc_then_double(5));\n}","solution":"fn compose(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32, g: impl Fn(i32) -> i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| g(f(x))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let inc_then_double = compose(|x| x + 1, |x| x * 2);\n    println!(\"{}\", inc_then_double(5));\n}","expect":"12","hints":["The new closure takes `f` and `g` with it: `move |x| ...`.","First `f(x)`, then pass the result to `g`: `g(f(x))`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `run_once` takes an `FnOnce() -> String` and prints the result. Pass it a closure that **hands out** the `report` string.","code":"fn run_once<F: FnOnce() -> String>(f: F) {\n    println!(\"{}\", f());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let report = String::from(\"report ready\");\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn run_once<F: FnOnce() -> String>(f: F) {\n    println!(\"{}\", f());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let report = String::from(\"report ready\");\n    run_once(move || report);\n}","expect":"report ready","hints":["The closure just returns `report`: `|| report`. You can add `move` for clarity."]},{"t":"task","md":"Build a \"calculator\": a `Vec<(&str, Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32>)>` with the operations `+`, `-`, `max`. Apply each to 8 and 3 and print `name: result`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = 8;\n    let b = 3;\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let a = 8;\n    let b = 3;\n    let ops: Vec<(&str, Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32>)> = vec![\n        (\"+\", Box::new(|x, y| x + y)),\n        (\"-\", Box::new(|x, y| x - y)),\n        (\"max\", Box::new(|x: i32, y| x.max(y))),\n    ];\n    for (name, f) in &ops {\n        println!(\"{name}: {}\", f(a, b));\n    }\n}","expect":"+: 11\n-: 5\nmax: 8","hints":["Each item is a pair `(\"name\", Box::new(|x, y| ...))`.","For `max` the compiler may need a type: `|x: i32, y| x.max(y)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `memo_square` returns an `FnMut(u64)` closure that computes a square and **counts its calls**. Print the result and the call count after three calls.","code":"fn memo_square() -> impl FnMut(u64) -> (u64, u32) {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut sq = memo_square();\n    sq(2);\n    sq(3);\n    let (v, calls) = sq(4);\n    println!(\"{v} {calls}\");\n}","solution":"fn memo_square() -> impl FnMut(u64) -> (u64, u32) {\n    let mut calls = 0;\n    move |x| {\n        calls += 1;\n        (x * x, calls)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut sq = memo_square();\n    sq(2);\n    sq(3);\n    let (v, calls) = sq(4);\n    println!(\"{v} {calls}\");\n}","expect":"16 3","hints":["Make `let mut calls = 0;` and return `move |x| { calls += 1; (x * x, calls) }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Combine functions — return `move |x| g(f(x))`.","A closure with its own state — `FnMut` + `move`.","A list of different operations — `Vec<Box<dyn Fn(...)>>`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use all three kinds of closures and return a closure from a function"]}]},{"id":"a04","title":"Errors in big programs","sub":"Your own error types, From and ?, Box<dyn Error>, anyhow and thiserror","lessons":[{"id":"a04-1","title":"Your own error type","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Why String is bad for errors","md":"In the basics we often wrote `Result<T, String>`. It's simple, but:\n- a string makes it hard to tell **which** error happened and react differently;\n- it's easy to make a typo in the text.\n\nBig programs describe errors with an `enum`. Each variant is one kind of problem.\n\n> **Analogy:** a doctor doesn't write \"something hurts\" in your chart. They write a diagnosis from a list. The diagnosis tells you how to treat it."},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum LoginError {\n    EmptyName,\n    TooShort(usize),\n    Banned { name: String },\n}\n\nfn check(name: &str) -> Result<(), LoginError> {\n    if name.is_empty() {\n        return Err(LoginError::EmptyName);\n    }\n    if name.len() < 3 {\n        return Err(LoginError::TooShort(name.len()));\n    }\n    if name == \"admin\" {\n        return Err(LoginError::Banned { name: name.to_string() });\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for n in [\"\", \"ab\", \"admin\", \"alice\"] {\n        println!(\"{:?}\", check(n));\n    }\n}","out":"Err(EmptyName)\nErr(TooShort(2))\nErr(Banned { name: \"admin\" })\nOk(())","after":"Variants can carry details: a length, a name — anything."},{"t":"text","title":"Clear text: Display","md":"`{:?}` is for programmers. People need `{}`. That comes from the `Display` trait:\n\n```\nuse std::fmt;\n\nimpl fmt::Display for LoginError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        match self {\n            LoginError::EmptyName => write!(f, \"the name is empty\"),\n            LoginError::TooShort(n) => write!(f, \"the name is short: {n} chars\"),\n            LoginError::Banned { name } => write!(f, \"{name} is banned\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"The Error trait","md":"The standard library has a trait `std::error::Error`. It says \"I am an error\". It requires `Debug` and `Display` and can itself be empty:\n\n```\nimpl std::error::Error for LoginError {}\n```\nWhy? Then your error can go into a `Box<dyn Error>`, be handed to other code, and be treated like any other Rust error."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which traits do you need to implement `std::error::Error`?","options":["Only `Clone`","`Debug` and `Display`","`From` and `Into`"],"answer":1,"why":["Errors don't need `Clone`.","Right. They are its supertraits.","Useful, but not required."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `Display` for `ParseColorError` so the program prints clear messages.","code":"use std::fmt;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum ParseColorError {\n    Empty,\n    Unknown(String),\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for ParseColorError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::error::Error for ParseColorError {}\n\nfn parse(s: &str) -> Result<&str, ParseColorError> {\n    match s {\n        \"\" => Err(ParseColorError::Empty),\n        \"red\" | \"green\" => Ok(s),\n        other => Err(ParseColorError::Unknown(other.to_string())),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"red\", \"\", \"pink\"] {\n        match parse(s) {\n            Ok(c) => println!(\"color: {c}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::fmt;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum ParseColorError {\n    Empty,\n    Unknown(String),\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for ParseColorError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        match self {\n            ParseColorError::Empty => write!(f, \"empty string\"),\n            ParseColorError::Unknown(s) => write!(f, \"unknown color \\\"{s}\\\"\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::error::Error for ParseColorError {}\n\nfn parse(s: &str) -> Result<&str, ParseColorError> {\n    match s {\n        \"\" => Err(ParseColorError::Empty),\n        \"red\" | \"green\" => Ok(s),\n        other => Err(ParseColorError::Unknown(other.to_string())),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"red\", \"\", \"pink\"] {\n        match parse(s) {\n            Ok(c) => println!(\"color: {c}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"color: red\nerror: empty string\nerror: unknown color \"pink\"","hints":["Inside `fmt`, write `match self { ... }` and `write!(f, \"...\")` in each branch.","A quote inside a string is written as `\\\"`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A big program's errors are an `enum`, one variant per kind.","`Display` is text for people, `Debug` is for programmers.","`impl std::error::Error for MyError {}` — \"I'm a real error\"."]}],"goals":["Describe all of a program's errors with one `enum`","Make an error print clearly"]},{"id":"a04-2","title":"From and ?: errors convert themselves","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Problem: errors of different kinds","md":"A function reads a number from text and checks it. `parse` can fail (a `ParseIntError`), and so can your check (an `AppError`). But a function can return only **one** error type."},{"t":"text","title":"The secret of ?","md":"`?` does a bit more than it seems. On error it calls `From::from(error)` — it **converts** it into the function's error type.\n\nSo it's enough to write `impl From<ParseIntError> for AppError`, and `?` will convert one error into the other by itself.\n\n> **Analogy:** a plug adapter. The plug is foreign, the socket is yours — the adapter (`From`) connects them."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt;\nuse std::num::ParseIntError;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AppError {\n    NotNumber(ParseIntError),\n    Negative(i32),\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for AppError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        match self {\n            AppError::NotNumber(e) => write!(f, \"not a number: {e}\"),\n            AppError::Negative(n) => write!(f, \"negative: {n}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl From<ParseIntError> for AppError {\n    fn from(e: ParseIntError) -> Self {\n        AppError::NotNumber(e)\n    }\n}\n\nfn read_positive(s: &str) -> Result<i32, AppError> {\n    let n: i32 = s.trim().parse()?; // ParseIntError -> AppError by itself\n    if n < 0 {\n        return Err(AppError::Negative(n));\n    }\n    Ok(n)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"42\", \"-5\", \"cat\"] {\n        match read_positive(s) {\n            Ok(n) => println!(\"ok {n}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"ok 42\nerror: negative: -5\nerror: not a number: invalid digit found in string","after":"Remove `impl From` and `?` stops compiling: Rust doesn't know how to turn one into the other."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `?` do when the function returns `Result<T, AppError>` and a call returned `Err(ParseIntError)`?","options":["Panics","Never compiles","Calls `AppError::from(...)` and returns the result"],"answer":2,"why":["No, there's no panic here.","It compiles if there's a matching `From`.","Right."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"You need `impl From<std::io::Error> for MyError` with a `from` method.","md":"Fill in the blanks so `?` can turn a `std::io::Error` into a `MyError`.","code":"impl ___<std::io::Error> for MyError {\n    fn ___(e: std::io::Error) -> Self {\n        MyError::Io(e)\n    }\n}","answers":[["From"],["from"]]},{"t":"text","title":"map_err: convert by hand","md":"Sometimes you don't want to write `From` — you need the error in one place only. Then use `map_err`:\n\n```\nlet n: i32 = s.parse().map_err(|_| AppError::Negative(0))?;\n```\n`map_err` changes the error, and `?` returns the finished one."},{"t":"task","md":"Write `impl From<ParseIntError> for AgeError` so that `?` in `parse_age` works.","code":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AgeError {\n    Bad(ParseIntError),\n    TooOld(u32),\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn parse_age(s: &str) -> Result<u32, AgeError> {\n    let n: u32 = s.parse()?;\n    if n > 150 {\n        return Err(AgeError::TooOld(n));\n    }\n    Ok(n)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"200\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", matches!(parse_age(\"x\"), Err(AgeError::Bad(_))));\n}","solution":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AgeError {\n    Bad(ParseIntError),\n    TooOld(u32),\n}\n\nimpl From<ParseIntError> for AgeError {\n    fn from(e: ParseIntError) -> Self {\n        AgeError::Bad(e)\n    }\n}\n\nfn parse_age(s: &str) -> Result<u32, AgeError> {\n    let n: u32 = s.parse()?;\n    if n > 150 {\n        return Err(AgeError::TooOld(n));\n    }\n    Ok(n)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"200\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", matches!(parse_age(\"x\"), Err(AgeError::Bad(_))));\n}","expect":"Ok(30)\nErr(TooOld(200))\ntrue","hints":["`impl From<ParseIntError> for AgeError { fn from(e: ParseIntError) -> Self { ... } }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`?` calls `From::from` on the error.","`impl From<TheirError> for MyError` — and `?` converts by itself.","For a one-off — `map_err(|e| ...)?`."]}],"goals":["See that `?` can change the error type","Gather errors from different places into one"]},{"id":"a04-3","title":"Box<dyn Error>: any error","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The lazy but honest option","md":"Sometimes your own `enum` is too much. For example, in a small script or in `main`. Then people write:\n\n```\nfn run() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> { ... }\n```\n`Box<dyn Error>` is \"a box with **some** error\". Any type with the `Error` trait turns into it through `?` by itself.\n\n> **Analogy:** one shared complaints box. Easy to drop things in, harder to sort them later."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::error::Error;\n\nfn sum_line(line: &str) -> Result<i32, Box<dyn Error>> {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for part in line.split('+') {\n        let n: i32 = part.trim().parse()?;\n        total += n;\n    }\n    if total > 100 {\n        return Err(\"too much\".into());\n    }\n    Ok(total)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", sum_line(\"1 + 2 + 3\").ok());\n    match sum_line(\"1 + x\") {\n        Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        Ok(_) => {}\n    }\n    match sum_line(\"60 + 70\") {\n        Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        Ok(_) => {}\n    }\n}","out":"Some(6)\nerror: invalid digit found in string\nerror: too much","after":"`\"too much\".into()` — even a plain string can become a `Box<dyn Error>`."},{"t":"text","title":"What to choose when","md":"- A **library** other people will use — your own `enum`. Let users `match` on the variants.\n- An **application** or a script where errors are mostly printed — `Box<dyn Error>` (or the `anyhow` crate, see the next lesson)."},{"t":"text","title":"main can return a Result too","md":"```\nfn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n    let n: i32 = \"42\".parse()?;\n    println!(\"{n}\");\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\nIf `main` returns `Err`, the program prints `Error: ...` and exits with an error code. Handy for short programs."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You're writing a library that reads settings. Other programs will use it. What do you pick for errors?","options":["Your own error `enum`","`Box<dyn Error>`","`String`"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. Users can tell the cases apart.","Users can't easily find out what exactly happened.","Even worse: just text."]},{"t":"deep","title":"The cause chain: source()","md":"A good error remembers **why** it happened. For that the `Error` trait has a `source()` method:\n\n```\nimpl std::error::Error for ConfigError {\n    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {\n        match self {\n            ConfigError::BadPort(e) => Some(e), // a ParseIntError inside\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nThat builds a chain: \"couldn't read the settings\" → \"the port is wrong\" → \"the string has a non-digit\". Loggers and `anyhow` can print the whole chain. That really helps find the cause of a problem."},{"t":"recap","items":["`Box<dyn Error>` — any error; `?` puts everything there by itself.","A library gets its own `enum`, an application gets `Box<dyn Error>` or `anyhow`.","`main` can return a `Result`."]}],"goals":["Return \"any error\" without your own `enum`","Learn about the `source()` cause chain"]},{"id":"a04-4","title":"thiserror and anyhow","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Two popular helpers","md":"We wrote `Display` and `From` by hand. That's a lot of repetitive code. Two crates do it for you:\n- **thiserror** — for **your own** error types. You write the `enum`, and `Display`, `Error` and `From` are generated from attributes.\n- **anyhow** — for **applications**. It's a handy `Box<dyn Error>` with extra context.\n\nIn your own project you add them to `Cargo.toml`. The Playground already has them."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"md":"thiserror: the error text sits right above the variant, `#[from]` — and `From` is done.","code":"use thiserror::Error;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Error)]\nenum AppError {\n    #[error(\"not a number: {0}\")]\n    NotNumber(#[from] std::num::ParseIntError),\n    #[error(\"negative: {0}\")]\n    Negative(i32),\n}\n\nfn read_positive(s: &str) -> Result<i32, AppError> {\n    let n: i32 = s.parse()?;\n    if n < 0 {\n        return Err(AppError::Negative(n));\n    }\n    Ok(n)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"7\", \"-1\", \"x\"] {\n        match read_positive(s) {\n            Ok(n) => println!(\"ok {n}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"ok 7\nerror: negative: -1\nerror: not a number: invalid digit found in string","after":"Compare with the last lesson: same result, a third of the code."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"md":"anyhow: any error plus clear context.","code":"use anyhow::{Context, Result, bail};\n\nfn parse_port(s: &str) -> Result<u16> {\n    let port: u16 = s.parse().context(\"the port must be a number\")?;\n    if port < 1024 {\n        bail!(\"port {port} is reserved\");\n    }\n    Ok(port)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"8080\", \"80\", \"http\"] {\n        match parse_port(s) {\n            Ok(p) => println!(\"port {p}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e:#}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"port 8080\nerror: port 80 is reserved\nerror: the port must be a number: invalid digit found in string","after":"- `.context(\"...\")` — add an explanation on top.\n- `bail!(...)` — return an error with a text right away.\n- `{e:#}` — print the whole cause chain separated by colons."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You're writing a command-line tool for yourself. Errors just need to be printed clearly. What's handier?","options":["thiserror","anyhow","Your own `enum` by hand"],"answer":1,"why":["It's good when someone will match on the error variants.","Right. Minimal code and clear context.","Possible, but slow and pointless here."]},{"t":"text","title":"An everyday rule","md":"- Writing a **library** → `thiserror`.\n- Writing a **program** → `anyhow`.\n- They are often used together: a library returns `thiserror` errors, and the app wraps them in `anyhow` with context."},{"t":"recap","items":["`thiserror`: `#[derive(Error)]`, `#[error(\"...\")]`, `#[from]`.","`anyhow`: `Result<T>`, `.context(...)`, `bail!`.","Library — thiserror, application — anyhow."]}],"goals":["Remove error boilerplate with crates","Know which crate is for what"]},{"id":"a04-p","title":"Practice: errors","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The function `withdraw` takes money from an account. Errors: `NotEnough { need, have }` and `Zero`. Write `Display` so the output matches.","code":"use std::fmt;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum BankError {\n    Zero,\n    NotEnough { need: u32, have: u32 },\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for BankError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn withdraw(balance: &mut u32, amount: u32) -> Result<(), BankError> {\n    if amount == 0 {\n        return Err(BankError::Zero);\n    }\n    if amount > *balance {\n        return Err(BankError::NotEnough { need: amount, have: *balance });\n    }\n    *balance -= amount;\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut b = 100;\n    for a in [30, 0, 500] {\n        match withdraw(&mut b, a) {\n            Ok(()) => println!(\"took {a}, left {b}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::fmt;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum BankError {\n    Zero,\n    NotEnough { need: u32, have: u32 },\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for BankError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        match self {\n            BankError::Zero => write!(f, \"the amount is zero\"),\n            BankError::NotEnough { need, have } => write!(f, \"need {need}, have {have}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn withdraw(balance: &mut u32, amount: u32) -> Result<(), BankError> {\n    if amount == 0 {\n        return Err(BankError::Zero);\n    }\n    if amount > *balance {\n        return Err(BankError::NotEnough { need: amount, have: *balance });\n    }\n    *balance -= amount;\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut b = 100;\n    for a in [30, 0, 500] {\n        match withdraw(&mut b, a) {\n            Ok(()) => println!(\"took {a}, left {b}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"error: {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"took 30, left 70\nerror: the amount is zero\nerror: need 500, have 70","hints":["A variant with fields is matched like this: `BankError::NotEnough { need, have } => write!(f, \"need {need}, have {have}\")`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `parse_point` parses the string `\"3,4\"` into a pair of numbers. Errors: `NoComma` and `Num(ParseIntError)`. Add a `From` so that `?` works.","code":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum PointError {\n    NoComma,\n    Num(ParseIntError),\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn parse_point(s: &str) -> Result<(i32, i32), PointError> {\n    let (a, b) = s.split_once(',').ok_or(PointError::NoComma)?;\n    Ok((a.trim().parse()?, b.trim().parse()?))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3, 4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3 4\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", matches!(parse_point(\"3,x\"), Err(PointError::Num(_))));\n}","solution":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum PointError {\n    NoComma,\n    Num(ParseIntError),\n}\n\nimpl From<ParseIntError> for PointError {\n    fn from(e: ParseIntError) -> Self {\n        PointError::Num(e)\n    }\n}\n\nfn parse_point(s: &str) -> Result<(i32, i32), PointError> {\n    let (a, b) = s.split_once(',').ok_or(PointError::NoComma)?;\n    Ok((a.trim().parse()?, b.trim().parse()?))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3, 4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3 4\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", matches!(parse_point(\"3,x\"), Err(PointError::Num(_))));\n}","expect":"Ok((3, 4))\nErr(NoComma)\ntrue","hints":["`ok_or(...)` turns an `Option` into a `Result`. And `parse()?` needs `impl From<ParseIntError> for PointError`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `total` adds up numbers from a list of strings and returns `Result<i32, Box<dyn Error>>`. If the sum is above 1000, it's the error `\"limit exceeded\"`.","code":"use std::error::Error;\n\nfn total(items: &[&str]) -> Result<i32, Box<dyn Error>> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", total(&[\"10\", \"20\"]).ok());\n    for bad in [&[\"10\", \"x\"][..], &[\"999\", \"2\"][..]] {\n        if let Err(e) = total(bad) {\n            println!(\"error: {e}\");\n        }\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::error::Error;\n\nfn total(items: &[&str]) -> Result<i32, Box<dyn Error>> {\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for s in items {\n        let n: i32 = s.parse()?;\n        sum += n;\n    }\n    if sum > 1000 {\n        return Err(\"limit exceeded\".into());\n    }\n    Ok(sum)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", total(&[\"10\", \"20\"]).ok());\n    for bad in [&[\"10\", \"x\"][..], &[\"999\", \"2\"][..]] {\n        if let Err(e) = total(bad) {\n            println!(\"error: {e}\");\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"Some(30)\nerror: invalid digit found in string\nerror: limit exceeded","hints":["Inside the loop `let n: i32 = s.parse()?;` — a parse error goes into the box by itself.","A text error is made like this: `Err(\"limit exceeded\".into())`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`enum` + `Display` + `From` — a complete error system.","`ok_or` turns an `Option` into a `Result` so you can use `?`.","`Box<dyn Error>` accepts any error, even strings."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Build an error system for a small program"]}]},{"id":"a05","title":"Lifetimes, deeper","sub":"Labels in impl, 'static, several labels, parsing strings without copies","lessons":[{"id":"a05-1","title":"Labels in structs and impl","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A short reminder","md":"A lifetime label `'a` **changes nothing** in the program. It only **describes** a link: \"this reference lives no longer than that one\".\n\n```\nfn longer<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {\n    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n```\nIt reads: \"the result lives while both arguments are alive\".\n\nIf you forgot the basics, see the Level 1 lesson \"Lifetimes\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a building pass that says \"valid as long as your escort's pass is valid\". The pass extends nothing; it just states the term honestly."},{"t":"text","title":"A struct with a reference","md":"If a struct holds a reference, it needs a label:\n\n```\nstruct Highlight<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n```\n\"`Highlight` can't outlive the string it looks at\".\n\nThe `impl` gets a label too — you declare it the same way you declare `<T>`:\n\n```\nimpl<'a> Highlight<'a> {\n    fn get(&self) -> &'a str { self.text }\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","code":"struct Highlight<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Highlight<'a> {\n    fn new(text: &'a str) -> Self {\n        Highlight { text }\n    }\n    fn first_word(&self) -> &'a str {\n        self.text.split_whitespace().next().unwrap_or(\"\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let book = String::from(\"Once upon a midnight dreary\");\n    let h = Highlight::new(&book);\n    println!(\"{}\", h.first_word());\n}","out":"Once","after":"`first_word` returns `&'a str` — a piece of the **original** book, not of the struct. It can outlive `h`, but not `book`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `struct View<'a> { s: &'a str }` mean?","options":["`View` lives exactly as long as the string","`View` can't outlive the string it refers to","The string is copied into `View`"],"answer":1,"why":["Not exactly: it may live shorter, never longer.","Right.","No, only a reference is inside."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"First the label is declared: `impl<'a>`, then used: `Parser<'a>`.","md":"Fill in the blanks in the `impl` declaration.","code":"struct Parser<'a> {\n    input: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<___> Parser<___> {\n    fn rest(&self) -> &'a str {\n        self.input\n    }\n}","answers":[["'a"],["'a"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["`'a` describes a link between terms, it doesn't change them.","A struct with a reference — `struct S<'a>`.","`impl<'a> S<'a>` — declare and use the label."]}],"goals":["Recall why the `'a` label exists","Write `impl<'a>` for a struct holding a reference"]},{"id":"a05-2","title":"'static: two different meanings","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Meaning one: a reference to something eternal","md":"`&'static str` is a reference that is valid **for the whole program**. String literals, for example: they are baked right into the program.\n\n```\nlet s: &'static str = \"hello\"; // the text lives in the program itself\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Meaning two: a type without temporary references","md":"`T: 'static` in a requirement does **not** mean \"lives forever\". It means: \"there are no references to anything temporary inside `T`\". Such a type can be kept as long as you like.\n\n- `String`, `i32`, `Vec<u8>` — fit: no references.\n- `&'static str` — fits.\n- `&'a str` with a short `'a` — doesn't fit.\n\n> **Analogy:** \"can be taken on a long hike\". What fits is what isn't tied to anything: your own backpack, yes; something borrowed for the afternoon, no."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn run_later<T: Send + 'static + std::fmt::Debug>(value: T) {\n    thread::spawn(move || println!(\"in the thread: {value:?}\")).join().unwrap();\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    run_later(String::from(\"my own string\")); // ok: String refers to nothing\n    run_later(vec![1, 2, 3]);                  // ok\n    run_later(\"a literal\");                    // ok: &'static str\n    // let local = String::from(\"x\");\n    // run_later(&local);                      // no: a reference to something temporary\n}","out":"in the thread: \"my own string\"\nin the thread: [1, 2, 3]\nin the thread: \"a literal\"","after":"`thread::spawn` requires `'static`: the thread may live longer than `main`. Uncomment the last lines and see the error."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Does `String` satisfy `T: 'static`?","options":["Yes","No, a `String` doesn't live forever","Only if the string is empty"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. There are no temporary references inside; `String` owns its data.","`'static` in a requirement isn't about forever, it's about having no temporary references.","The contents don't matter."]},{"t":"text","title":"The compiler asks for 'static. What now?","md":"A common situation: \"borrowed value does not live long enough… argument requires that it is borrowed for `'static`\".\n\nUsually one of these helps:\n- pass **ownership** (`String` instead of `&str`, `move` in a closure);\n- clone the data;\n- wrap it in an `Arc` (see the threads module).\n\nYou almost never need to write `'static` in your own labels just to \"silence\" the compiler."},{"t":"recap","items":["`&'static T` — a reference to something that lives the whole program (literals).","`T: 'static` — the type has no temporary references. `String`, `Vec`, numbers fit.","Asked for `'static`? Give ownership or clone."]}],"goals":["Tell `&'static T` from `T: 'static`","Not be scared of `'static` in compiler errors"]},{"id":"a05-3","title":"Label elision rules","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Three rules","md":"The compiler fills in labels by itself using three rules:\n1. Each reference argument gets **its own** label.\n2. If there is **one** reference argument, its label goes to the result.\n3. If there is `&self` or `&mut self`, the result gets **`self`'s** label.\n\nIf the rules don't settle it, write the labels yourself."},{"t":"code","code":"// Rule 2: one reference argument — the result lives like it\nfn first(s: &str) -> &str {\n    &s[..1]\n}\n\nstruct Doc {\n    title: String,\n}\n\nimpl Doc {\n    // Rule 3: the result lives like self\n    fn short(&self, n: usize) -> &str {\n        &self.title[..n]\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", first(\"Rust\"));\n    let d = Doc { title: String::from(\"Manual\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", d.short(3));\n}","out":"R\nMan","after":"Not a single label, and it all works: the compiler applied the rules."},{"t":"text","title":"When the rules guess wrong","md":"A method with `&self` returns a piece of **another** argument — rule 3 gets it wrong:\n\n```\nimpl Doc {\n    // without labels the compiler would decide the result lives like self\n    fn pick<'b>(&self, other: &'b str) -> &'b str {\n        other\n    }\n}\n```\nHere you write the label explicitly: the result is tied to `other`, not to `self`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"`fn f(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str` without labels. Will it compile?","options":["Yes, by rule 2","No, the compiler doesn't know what the result is tied to","Yes, by rule 3"],"answer":1,"why":["Rule 2 is for **one** reference, and there are two here.","Right. You need `<'a>` explicitly.","There's no `self` here."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `prefix` returns the part of `text` whose length is `pattern.len()`. Add labels so the result depends only on `text`.","code":"fn prefix(text: &str, pattern: &str) -> &str {\n    &text[..pattern.len()]\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"hello world\");\n    let p;\n    {\n        let pattern = String::from(\"hey\");\n        p = prefix(&text, &pattern);\n    }\n    println!(\"{p}\");\n}","solution":"fn prefix<'a>(text: &'a str, pattern: &str) -> &'a str {\n    &text[..pattern.len()]\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"hello world\");\n    let p;\n    {\n        let pattern = String::from(\"hey\");\n        p = prefix(&text, &pattern);\n    }\n    println!(\"{p}\");\n}","expect":"hel","hints":["Only `text` and the result need the label: `fn prefix<'a>(text: &'a str, pattern: &str) -> &'a str`.","If you also tie the result to `pattern`, `main` won't compile: `pattern` dies before `println!`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"for<'a>: a label \"for any\"","md":"Sometimes you need to say: \"a closure that works with a reference of **any** term\". There's a notation for that, `for<'a>`:\n\n```\nfn apply<F>(f: F) where F: for<'a> Fn(&'a str) -> &'a str { ... }\n```\nIt reads: \"for any `'a`, the function takes `&'a str` and returns `&'a str`\".\n\nGood news: you usually don't need to write it. The compiler expands `F: Fn(&str) -> &str` into `for<'a>` by itself. But this notation sometimes shows up in error messages, so it's good to recognize it. It's called **higher-ranked trait bounds** (HRTB)."},{"t":"recap","items":["One reference in — the result lives like it.","There's `&self` — the result lives like `self`.","Otherwise write the labels yourself and tie the result only to where it comes from."]}],"goals":["Know when labels can be left out","Understand where a method's result gets its term"]},{"id":"a05-4","title":"Parsing a string without copies","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Why return &str and not String","md":"You're splitting a big text into words. You could make each word a new `String` — that's **copying**. Or you could return a `&str` — a **window** into the original text. Zero copies.\n\nLifetime labels are exactly what makes this safe: Rust makes sure the windows don't outlive the text.\n\n> **Analogy:** bookmarks in a book instead of photocopies of pages. Bookmarks weigh nothing, but they're useless without the book."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Tokens<'a> {\n    rest: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for Tokens<'a> {\n    type Item = &'a str;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a str> {\n        let s = self.rest.trim_start();\n        if s.is_empty() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let end = s.find(' ').unwrap_or(s.len());\n        let (word, rest) = s.split_at(end);\n        self.rest = rest;\n        Some(word)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let line = String::from(\"  let x  = 42 ;\");\n    let words: Vec<&str> = Tokens { rest: &line }.collect();\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","out":"[\"let\", \"x\", \"=\", \"42\", \";\"]","after":"Not a single new string: each word is a slice of `line`."},{"t":"text","title":"Where the labels are","md":"- `Tokens<'a>` holds `&'a str` — a window into the text.\n- `type Item = &'a str` — yields pieces of **the same** text, not of the iterator.\n- So `words` can live longer than the iterator — but not longer than `line`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why does `Tokens` say `type Item = &'a str` and not just `&str`?","options":["It must say the words are pieces of the original text with term `'a`","It runs faster that way","Without the label the words would be copied"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. You can't leave a label out in an associated type.","Labels don't affect speed.","There are no copies either way — it just won't compile without the label."]},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the `key_value` method, which parses `\"key=value\"` and returns a pair of pieces of the **original** string.","code":"struct Line<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Line<'a> {\n    fn key_value(&self) -> Option<(&'a str, &'a str)> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let raw = String::from(\"name=Rust\");\n    let pair = Line { text: &raw }.key_value();\n    println!(\"{pair:?}\");\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Line { text: \"no sign\" }.key_value());\n}","solution":"struct Line<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Line<'a> {\n    fn key_value(&self) -> Option<(&'a str, &'a str)> {\n        let (k, v) = self.text.split_once('=')?;\n        Some((k.trim(), v.trim()))\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let raw = String::from(\"name=Rust\");\n    let pair = Line { text: &raw }.key_value();\n    println!(\"{pair:?}\");\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Line { text: \"no sign\" }.key_value());\n}","expect":"Some((\"name\", \"Rust\"))\nNone","hints":["`split_once('=')` returns `Option<(&str, &str)>`. The `?` sign works with `Option` too."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Return `&'a str` to avoid copying text.","The label in `type Item = &'a str` ties the result to the text, not the iterator.","Rust won't let the windows outlive the text."]}],"goals":["Write a parser that returns pieces of the original string","See why it's fast"]},{"id":"a05-p","title":"Practice: lifetimes","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Longest` struct remembers the longest word seen so far. Add labels so the code compiles.","code":"struct Longest {\n    best: &str,\n}\n\nimpl Longest {\n    fn see(&mut self, w: &str) {\n        if w.len() > self.best.len() {\n            self.best = w;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"cat hippopotamus hedgehog\");\n    let mut l = Longest { best: \"\" };\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        l.see(w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", l.best);\n}","solution":"struct Longest<'a> {\n    best: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Longest<'a> {\n    fn see(&mut self, w: &'a str) {\n        if w.len() > self.best.len() {\n            self.best = w;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"cat hippopotamus hedgehog\");\n    let mut l = Longest { best: \"\" };\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        l.see(w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", l.best);\n}","expect":"hippopotamus","hints":["The struct: `struct Longest<'a> { best: &'a str }`, and `impl<'a> Longest<'a>`.","`w` gets stored in `best`, so it has the same term: `w: &'a str`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `split_name` splits `\"First Last\"` into two parts without copying. Write its signature and body.","code":"// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let full = String::from(\"Ada Lovelace\");\n    let (first, last) = split_name(&full);\n    println!(\"{last}, {first}\");\n}","solution":"fn split_name(full: &str) -> (&str, &str) {\n    full.split_once(' ').unwrap_or((full, \"\"))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let full = String::from(\"Ada Lovelace\");\n    let (first, last) = split_name(&full);\n    println!(\"{last}, {first}\");\n}","expect":"Lovelace, Ada","hints":["There's one reference argument, so you can skip the labels: `fn split_name(full: &str) -> (&str, &str)`.","`split_once(' ')` returns an `Option`. If there's no space — `unwrap_or((full, \"\"))`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `pick` takes two texts and a flag. If the flag is `true`, it returns the first, otherwise the second. Add labels.","code":"fn pick(a: &str, b: &str, first: bool) -> &str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x = String::from(\"left\");\n    let y = String::from(\"right\");\n    println!(\"{}\", pick(&x, &y, false));\n}","solution":"fn pick<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str, first: bool) -> &'a str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x = String::from(\"left\");\n    let y = String::from(\"right\");\n    println!(\"{}\", pick(&x, &y, false));\n}","expect":"right","hints":["The result may be either of the two, so both get the same label `'a`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A struct that holds a reference gets a label.","If you store an argument in a field, the argument gets the same label.","A result that is \"one of two\" — one shared label."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Place labels in a few typical spots"]}]},{"id":"a06","title":"Memory without surprises","sub":"Drop, mem::take, Cow and Weak","lessons":[{"id":"a06-1","title":"Drop: what happens at the end","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Scheduled cleanup","md":"When an owner goes out of scope, Rust **drops** the value. Before that it calls the `drop` method of the `Drop` trait, if there is one.\n\nThat's how a `String` frees its memory, a file gets closed and a lock gets released. You don't call `drop` yourself — Rust does.\n\n> **Analogy:** checking out of a hotel. You just leave, and housekeeping cleans the room. Your own `Drop` is a note for housekeeping: \"please water the plant\"."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Noisy(&'static str);\n\nimpl Drop for Noisy {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"cleaning up {}\", self.0);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let _a = Noisy(\"a\");\n    let _b = Noisy(\"b\");\n    {\n        let _c = Noisy(\"c\");\n        println!(\"end of block\");\n    }\n    println!(\"end of main\");\n}","out":"end of block\ncleaning up c\nend of main\ncleaning up b\ncleaning up a","after":"Variables are dropped in **reverse** order: the last one created goes first. Like plates in a stack."},{"t":"text","title":"Drop it early: drop(x)","md":"Sometimes a value must go **before** the end of the block: close a file, release a lock. That's what the `drop(x)` function is for:\n\n```\nlet lock = data.lock().unwrap();\n// work with the data\ndrop(lock); // released the lock now, not at the end of the function\n```\n`drop(x)` simply takes `x` and ends right away. Rust won't let you call `x.drop()` directly — so the same thing can't be cleaned up twice."},{"t":"quiz","md":"`let a = ...; let b = ...;` — in what order does Rust drop them at the end of the block?","options":["First `a`, then `b`","First `b`, then `a`","At the same time"],"answer":1,"why":["The other way round.","Right. Reverse order of creation.","The order is always strict."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"How do you drop a value `x` before the end of the block?","options":["`x.drop()`","`drop(x)`","`x = null`"],"answer":1,"why":["Rust doesn't allow that.","Right.","Rust has no `null`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Field order and why _ is dangerous","md":"Struct fields are dropped in **declaration** order — top to bottom. This rarely matters, but sometimes one field must die before another (say, a thread before a channel). Then you reorder the fields.\n\nAnother trap: `let _ = value;` drops the value **immediately**, because `_` isn't a variable, it means \"throw away\". But `let _guard = value;` keeps it until the end of the block. With locks the difference is huge:\n\n```\nlet _ = mutex.lock(); // the lock is released right away!\nlet _g = mutex.lock(); // the lock is held until the end of the block\n```"},{"t":"task","md":"Write `Drop` for `Guard` so it prints `leaving: <name>`. Check the order.","code":"struct Guard {\n    name: String,\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let _outer = Guard { name: String::from(\"outer\") };\n    let inner = Guard { name: String::from(\"inner\") };\n    drop(inner);\n    println!(\"working\");\n}","solution":"struct Guard {\n    name: String,\n}\n\nimpl Drop for Guard {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"leaving: {}\", self.name);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let _outer = Guard { name: String::from(\"outer\") };\n    let inner = Guard { name: String::from(\"inner\") };\n    drop(inner);\n    println!(\"working\");\n}","expect":"leaving: inner\nworking\nleaving: outer","hints":["`impl Drop for Guard { fn drop(&mut self) { println!(\"leaving: {}\", self.name); } }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`Drop::drop` is called by itself when a value is dropped.","Variables are dropped in reverse order.","`drop(x)` — clean up early."]}],"goals":["Learn the order in which values are dropped","Write your own `Drop`"]},{"id":"a06-2","title":"mem::take and mem::replace","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Problem: taking from behind a reference","md":"You have `&mut self` and need to **take** the field `self.items` (say, to hand it out). Plain `let v = self.items;` isn't allowed: you can't leave the struct with a \"hole\".\n\nThe fix is to swap the value for another one:\n- `std::mem::take(&mut x)` — take `x` and put an empty (default) value in its place.\n- `std::mem::replace(&mut x, new)` — take `x` and put `new` in its place.\n- `std::mem::swap(&mut a, &mut b)` — swap two values.\n\n> **Analogy:** swapping the vase on a table. You take the old one and put the new one down at once — the table is never empty."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::mem;\n\nstruct Inbox {\n    messages: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Inbox {\n    fn drain_all(&mut self) -> Vec<String> {\n        mem::take(&mut self.messages) // taken, an empty Vec in its place\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut inbox = Inbox { messages: vec![String::from(\"hi\"), String::from(\"how are you\")] };\n    let got = inbox.drain_all();\n    println!(\"took {}: {:?}\", got.len(), got);\n    println!(\"in the inbox: {}\", inbox.messages.len());\n\n    let mut mode = String::from(\"day\");\n    let old = mem::replace(&mut mode, String::from(\"night\"));\n    println!(\"{old} -> {mode}\");\n}","out":"took 2: [\"hi\", \"how are you\"]\nin the inbox: 0\nday -> night","after":"No `clone()` at all: the strings simply moved."},{"t":"text","title":"take and Option","md":"`Option` has a handy `take()` method: it takes the value and leaves `None`.\n\n```\nlet mut slot: Option<String> = Some(String::from(\"key\"));\nlet k = slot.take(); // k = Some(\"key\"), slot = None\n```\n\"One-time\" things are often built this way: take it, and it's gone."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What's left in `v` after `let x = std::mem::take(&mut v);`, if `v: Vec<i32>`?","options":["An empty vector","Nothing, `v` was dropped","A copy of the old vector"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. `take` puts the default value there.","`v` is alive, just empty.","There are no copies, everything moved into `x`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `finish` method must return the collected text and clear the buffer. Use `mem::take`.","code":"use std::mem;\n\nstruct Writer {\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl Writer {\n    fn write(&mut self, s: &str) {\n        self.buf.push_str(s);\n    }\n    fn finish(&mut self) -> String {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut w = Writer { buf: String::new() };\n    w.write(\"One, \");\n    w.write(\"two\");\n    let text = w.finish();\n    println!(\"{text}\");\n    println!(\"empty: {}\", w.buf.is_empty());\n}","solution":"use std::mem;\n\nstruct Writer {\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl Writer {\n    fn write(&mut self, s: &str) {\n        self.buf.push_str(s);\n    }\n    fn finish(&mut self) -> String {\n        mem::take(&mut self.buf)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut w = Writer { buf: String::new() };\n    w.write(\"One, \");\n    w.write(\"two\");\n    let text = w.finish();\n    println!(\"{text}\");\n    println!(\"empty: {}\", w.buf.is_empty());\n}","expect":"One, two\nempty: true","hints":["One line: `mem::take(&mut self.buf)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`mem::take` — take it and leave an empty one.","`mem::replace` — take it and leave your own.","`Option::take()` — take it and leave `None`."]}],"goals":["Get a value out of a `&mut` without cloning","Know `take`, `replace` and `swap`"]},{"id":"a06-3","title":"Cow: copy only when needed","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Sometimes change, sometimes not","md":"A function cleans up text: it removes extra whitespace. Often the text is **already** clean — no reason to copy it. But sometimes it must change — and then you need a new `String`.\n\n`Cow` (clone on write) is an `enum` with two variants:\n- `Cow::Borrowed(&str)` — just a reference, nothing copied;\n- `Cow::Owned(String)` — we had to make our own copy.\n\n> **Analogy:** borrowing a book from a friend. If you only read it, you give it back as is. Want to underline things? Buy your own."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::borrow::Cow;\n\nfn no_tabs(s: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {\n    if s.contains('\\t') {\n        Cow::Owned(s.replace('\\t', \"    \"))\n    } else {\n        Cow::Borrowed(s)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for line in [\"clean line\", \"a\\tb\"] {\n        let r = no_tabs(line);\n        let kind = match r {\n            Cow::Borrowed(_) => \"no copy\",\n            Cow::Owned(_) => \"copy\",\n        };\n        println!(\"{kind}: {r}\");\n    }\n}","out":"no copy: clean line\ncopy: a    b","after":"A `Cow` prints and works like a normal string. The only difference is whether a copy was made."},{"t":"text","title":"Where Cow shows up","md":"- `String::from_utf8_lossy` returns a `Cow<str>`: if the bytes are valid, there's no copy.\n- In functions that \"usually change nothing\".\n- `.into_owned()` turns any `Cow` into an owned value when you definitely need to own it."},{"t":"quiz","md":"A function returned `Cow::Borrowed(s)`. Was anything copied?","options":["Yes, `Cow` always copies","No, it's just a reference to the original data","Unknown"],"answer":1,"why":["It copies only on write.","Right.","The `Borrowed` variant says it plainly: no copy."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `shout` returns the text as is if it's already all uppercase, otherwise a new uppercase string. Use `Cow`.","code":"use std::borrow::Cow;\n\nfn shout(s: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"HELLO\", \"Hi\"] {\n        let r = shout(s);\n        println!(\"{} {}\", r, matches!(r, Cow::Borrowed(_)));\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::borrow::Cow;\n\nfn shout(s: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {\n    if s.chars().all(|c| !c.is_lowercase()) {\n        Cow::Borrowed(s)\n    } else {\n        Cow::Owned(s.to_uppercase())\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"HELLO\", \"Hi\"] {\n        let r = shout(s);\n        println!(\"{} {}\", r, matches!(r, Cow::Borrowed(_)));\n    }\n}","expect":"HELLO true\nHI false","hints":["The check is \"no lowercase letters\": `s.chars().all(|c| !c.is_lowercase())`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`Cow` = either a reference (`Borrowed`) or an owned value (`Owned`).","Handy when changes are rarely needed.","`.into_owned()` — get an owned value either way."]}],"goals":["Understand what `Cow` is","Return either a reference or a new string from the same function"]},{"id":"a06-4","title":"Weak: a reference that doesn't hold on","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A leak through a circle","md":"`Rc` drops a value when its owner count reaches zero. What if A holds B and B holds A? The counts never reach zero, so the memory is never freed. That's a **leak**.\n\n> **Analogy:** two people holding hands, each waiting for the other to let go first. They stand there forever."},{"t":"text","title":"Weak — look without holding","md":"`Weak` is a weak reference. It knows where the value is but **doesn't extend** its life.\n- `Rc::downgrade(&rc)` — make a `Weak` from an `Rc`.\n- `weak.upgrade()` — try to get an `Rc` back. Returns an `Option`: `None` if the value is already gone.\n\nThe rule: **a parent holds its children with `Rc`, a child looks at its parent with `Weak`**."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::rc::{Rc, Weak};\n\nstruct Node {\n    name: String,\n    parent: RefCell<Weak<Node>>,\n    children: RefCell<Vec<Rc<Node>>>,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let root = Rc::new(Node {\n        name: String::from(\"root\"),\n        parent: RefCell::new(Weak::new()),\n        children: RefCell::new(vec![]),\n    });\n    let leaf = Rc::new(Node {\n        name: String::from(\"leaf\"),\n        parent: RefCell::new(Weak::new()),\n        children: RefCell::new(vec![]),\n    });\n    *leaf.parent.borrow_mut() = Rc::downgrade(&root);\n    root.children.borrow_mut().push(Rc::clone(&leaf));\n\n    let p = leaf.parent.borrow().upgrade();\n    println!(\"leaf's parent: {}\", p.map(|n| n.name.clone()).unwrap_or_default());\n    println!(\"root's children: {}\", root.children.borrow().len());\n    println!(\"root strong: {}, weak: {}\", Rc::strong_count(&root), Rc::weak_count(&root));\n}","out":"leaf's parent: root\nroot's children: 1\nroot strong: 1, weak: 1","after":"The root has one strong owner (`root`) and one weak reference (from the leaf). When `root` goes away, the root node is dropped — no cycle."},{"t":"code","md":"`upgrade` returns `None` if the value is already gone:","code":"use std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn main() {\n    let strong = Rc::new(5);\n    let weak = Rc::downgrade(&strong);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", weak.upgrade());\n    drop(strong);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", weak.upgrade());\n}","out":"Some(5)\nNone"},{"t":"quiz","md":"In a tree, a child keeps a reference to its parent. Which kind?","options":["`Rc<Node>`","`Weak<Node>`","`Box<Node>`"],"answer":1,"why":["That makes a cycle: the parent holds the child, the child holds the parent.","Right. The child looks but doesn't hold.","`Box` is the only owner, and the parent already has an owner."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"`downgrade` makes a weak reference, `upgrade` tries to get a strong one again.","md":"Fill in the blanks: get a `Weak` from an `Rc`, then try to get the `Rc` back.","code":"let strong = Rc::new(String::from(\"data\"));\nlet weak = Rc::___(&strong);\nif let Some(s) = weak.___() {\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n}","answers":[["downgrade"],["upgrade"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["A circle of `Rc`s is a leak: the counts never reach zero.","`Weak` looks but doesn't hold. `upgrade()` → `Option<Rc>`.","Parent → child: `Rc`. Child → parent: `Weak`."]}],"goals":["See how `Rc` can leak memory","Break a cycle with `Weak`"]},{"id":"a06-p","title":"Practice: memory","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"A traffic light's state changes with the `next` method, which returns the **old** state. Use `mem::replace`.","code":"use std::mem;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Light {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Yellow,\n}\n\nstruct Traffic {\n    light: Light,\n}\n\nimpl Traffic {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Light {\n        let new = match self.light {\n            Light::Red => Light::Green,\n            Light::Green => Light::Yellow,\n            Light::Yellow => Light::Red,\n        };\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Traffic { light: Light::Red };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.next());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.next());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.light);\n}","solution":"use std::mem;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Light {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Yellow,\n}\n\nstruct Traffic {\n    light: Light,\n}\n\nimpl Traffic {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Light {\n        let new = match self.light {\n            Light::Red => Light::Green,\n            Light::Green => Light::Yellow,\n            Light::Yellow => Light::Red,\n        };\n        mem::replace(&mut self.light, new)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Traffic { light: Light::Red };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.next());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.next());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.light);\n}","expect":"Red\nGreen\nYellow","hints":["`mem::replace(&mut self.light, new)` puts `new` in and returns the old one."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Tracker` counter prints how many times it was called when it's dropped. Write the `Drop`.","code":"struct Tracker {\n    calls: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Tracker {\n    fn hit(&mut self) {\n        self.calls += 1;\n    }\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Tracker { calls: 0 };\n    t.hit();\n    t.hit();\n    t.hit();\n    println!(\"done\");\n}","solution":"struct Tracker {\n    calls: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Tracker {\n    fn hit(&mut self) {\n        self.calls += 1;\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Drop for Tracker {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"calls: {}\", self.calls);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Tracker { calls: 0 };\n    t.hit();\n    t.hit();\n    t.hit();\n    println!(\"done\");\n}","expect":"done\ncalls: 3","hints":["The message appears after `done`: `t` is dropped at the end of `main`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `normalize` replaces `colour` with `color`. If there's no `colour`, no copy is made. Count how many strings had to be copied.","code":"use std::borrow::Cow;\n\nfn normalize(s: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = [\"colour\", \"cat\", \"colourful\", \"dog\"];\n    let mut copies = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        let n = normalize(w);\n        if let Cow::Owned(_) = n {\n            copies += 1;\n        }\n        print!(\"{n} \");\n    }\n    println!();\n    println!(\"copies: {copies}\");\n}","solution":"use std::borrow::Cow;\n\nfn normalize(s: &str) -> Cow<'_, str> {\n    if s.contains(\"colour\") {\n        Cow::Owned(s.replace(\"colour\", \"color\"))\n    } else {\n        Cow::Borrowed(s)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = [\"colour\", \"cat\", \"colourful\", \"dog\"];\n    let mut copies = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        let n = normalize(w);\n        if let Cow::Owned(_) = n {\n            copies += 1;\n        }\n        print!(\"{n} \");\n    }\n    println!();\n    println!(\"copies: {copies}\");\n}","expect":"color cat colorful dog\ncopies: 2","hints":["`s.contains(\"colour\")` checks, `s.replace(\"colour\", \"color\")` makes the new string."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`mem::replace` — switch the state and return the old one.","`Drop` — code \"on the way out\".","`Cow` — copy only what changes."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use `Drop`, `mem::replace`, `Cow` and `Weak`"]}]},{"id":"a07","title":"Patterns with types","sub":"Newtype, builder, state machine and typestate","lessons":[{"id":"a07-1","title":"Newtype: a wrapper with character","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Two f64s are easy to mix up","md":"```\nfn travel(distance: f64, time: f64) -> f64 { distance / time }\ntravel(2.0, 100.0); // swapped by mistake — the compiler says nothing\n```\nBoth arguments are `f64`. Rust doesn't know these are meters and seconds.\n\nA **newtype** is a struct with one field: `struct Meters(f64);`. Inside it's the same number, but the type is **different**. Now you can't mix them up.\n\n> **Analogy:** identical jars in a kitchen. Without labels, it's easy to mistake salt for sugar."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Meters(f64);\nstruct Seconds(f64);\n\nfn speed(d: Meters, t: Seconds) -> f64 {\n    d.0 / t.0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let d = Meters(100.0);\n    let t = Seconds(20.0);\n    println!(\"{} m/s\", speed(d, t));\n    // speed(Seconds(20.0), Meters(100.0)); // error: wrong types\n}","out":"5 m/s","after":"Inside it's a normal number, and the speed is the same: the wrapper disappears at compile time."},{"t":"text","title":"Check once","md":"A newtype with a **private** field and a constructor that checks the data. Then it's simply impossible to create a wrong value.\n\n```\npub struct Email(String);\n\nimpl Email {\n    pub fn parse(s: &str) -> Option<Email> {\n        if s.contains('@') { Some(Email(s.to_string())) } else { None }\n    }\n}\n```\nAny function that takes an `Email` doesn't need to check it again: if it exists, it's valid."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why write `struct UserId(u64)` instead of just using `u64`?","options":["It runs faster","So an id can't be mixed up with other numbers","`u64` can't be stored in structs"],"answer":1,"why":["The speed is the same.","Right. The compiler now tells them apart.","It can."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make a newtype `Percent(u8)` with a `new` constructor that returns `None` if the number is above 100.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Percent(u8);\n\nimpl Percent {\n    fn new(v: u8) -> Option<Percent> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Percent::new(42));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Percent::new(150));\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Percent(u8);\n\nimpl Percent {\n    fn new(v: u8) -> Option<Percent> {\n        if v <= 100 { Some(Percent(v)) } else { None }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Percent::new(42));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", Percent::new(150));\n}","expect":"Some(Percent(42))\nNone","hints":["`if v <= 100 { Some(Percent(v)) } else { None }`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Deref: handy, but careful","md":"Sometimes you want the wrapper to behave like what's inside: `name.len()` instead of `name.0.len()`. For that you implement the `Deref` trait:\n\n```\nimpl std::ops::Deref for Name {\n    type Target = str;\n    fn deref(&self) -> &str { &self.0 }\n}\n```\nBut many people advise against overusing it: a newtype exists precisely to be **different** from the inner type. If the wrapper behaves exactly the same, the point is lost. It's better to write the methods you need by hand."},{"t":"recap","items":["A newtype is `struct Name(Type);` — the same type inside, a different one outside.","It protects against mixing up values.","With a private field and a check in the constructor, a wrong value can't be created."]}],"goals":["Wrap a simple type so values don't get mixed up","Check data once — when it's created"]},{"id":"a07-2","title":"Builder","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Problem: many settings","md":"A request has ten settings, and almost all of them are optional. A function `new(a, b, c, d, e, f…)` is a nightmare: easy to mix up the order, and writing `None, None, None` is boring.\n\nA **builder** is a separate struct that collects settings one by one and makes the finished object at the end.\n\n> **Analogy:** ordering a burger. \"No onions. Double cheese. That's it.\" Each phrase is one call."},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Request {\n    url: String,\n    method: String,\n    timeout: u32,\n    retries: u32,\n}\n\nstruct RequestBuilder {\n    url: String,\n    method: String,\n    timeout: u32,\n    retries: u32,\n}\n\nimpl RequestBuilder {\n    fn new(url: &str) -> Self {\n        RequestBuilder { url: url.to_string(), method: String::from(\"GET\"), timeout: 30, retries: 0 }\n    }\n    fn method(mut self, m: &str) -> Self {\n        self.method = m.to_string();\n        self\n    }\n    fn timeout(mut self, t: u32) -> Self {\n        self.timeout = t;\n        self\n    }\n    fn retries(mut self, r: u32) -> Self {\n        self.retries = r;\n        self\n    }\n    fn build(self) -> Request {\n        Request { url: self.url, method: self.method, timeout: self.timeout, retries: self.retries }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let r = RequestBuilder::new(\"rust-lang.org\").method(\"POST\").retries(3).build();\n    println!(\"{r:?}\");\n}","out":"Request { url: \"rust-lang.org\", method: \"POST\", timeout: 30, retries: 3 }","after":"Each method **takes** the builder (`mut self`) and **returns** it. That's why they can be chained."},{"t":"text","title":"Even simpler: Default","md":"If every field has a sensible default, sometimes `#[derive(Default)]` and the \"rest by default\" syntax are enough:\n\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\nstruct Config { verbose: bool, level: u8, name: String }\n\nlet c = Config { level: 3, ..Default::default() };\n```\n`..Default::default()` means \"take all the other fields from the default\"."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why are builder methods written as `fn timeout(mut self, t: u32) -> Self`?","options":["So you can chain them: `.a().b().c()`","The compiler requires it","So the builder can't be used"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. Each call returns the builder for the next one.","It's a style choice, not a requirement.","The opposite: so it's convenient to use."]},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the pizza builder: a `size` method and a `topping` method that adds a topping to the list.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pizza {\n    size: u32,\n    toppings: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nstruct PizzaBuilder {\n    size: u32,\n    toppings: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl PizzaBuilder {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        PizzaBuilder { size: 30, toppings: vec![] }\n    }\n    // your code\n    fn build(self) -> Pizza {\n        Pizza { size: self.size, toppings: self.toppings }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = PizzaBuilder::new().size(40).topping(\"cheese\").topping(\"mushrooms\").build();\n    println!(\"{p:?}\");\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pizza {\n    size: u32,\n    toppings: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nstruct PizzaBuilder {\n    size: u32,\n    toppings: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl PizzaBuilder {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        PizzaBuilder { size: 30, toppings: vec![] }\n    }\n    fn size(mut self, s: u32) -> Self {\n        self.size = s;\n        self\n    }\n    fn topping(mut self, t: &str) -> Self {\n        self.toppings.push(t.to_string());\n        self\n    }\n    fn build(self) -> Pizza {\n        Pizza { size: self.size, toppings: self.toppings }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = PizzaBuilder::new().size(40).topping(\"cheese\").topping(\"mushrooms\").build();\n    println!(\"{p:?}\");\n}","expect":"Pizza { size: 40, toppings: [\"cheese\", \"mushrooms\"] }","hints":["`fn size(mut self, s: u32) -> Self { self.size = s; self }`.","`topping` does `self.toppings.push(t.to_string())` and returns `self`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A builder collects settings one by one; `build()` makes the object.","Methods: `fn x(mut self, ...) -> Self` — for chaining.","Simple cases — `..Default::default()`."]}],"goals":["Create objects with lots of optional settings","Write chains like `.with_...().with_...()`"]},{"id":"a07-3","title":"A state machine with enum","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"States and transitions","md":"An order can be created, paid, shipped, delivered. From \"created\" you can go to \"paid\", but not straight to \"delivered\".\n\nThis is a **state machine**. In Rust it's convenient to write one with an `enum`: each state is a variant, and data needed only in that state lives right in the variant.\n\n> **Analogy:** a board game. A token stands on a square, and a move takes it to another one according to the rules."},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Order {\n    Created,\n    Paid { amount: u32 },\n    Shipped { amount: u32, track: String },\n    Delivered,\n}\n\nimpl Order {\n    fn next(self) -> Order {\n        match self {\n            Order::Created => Order::Paid { amount: 500 },\n            Order::Paid { amount } => Order::Shipped { amount, track: String::from(\"US123\") },\n            Order::Shipped { .. } => Order::Delivered,\n            Order::Delivered => Order::Delivered,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut o = Order::Created;\n    for _ in 0..4 {\n        println!(\"{o:?}\");\n        o = o.next();\n    }\n}","out":"Created\nPaid { amount: 500 }\nShipped { amount: 500, track: \"US123\" }\nDelivered","after":"Only a shipped order has a tracking number. In other states it simply doesn't exist — so it can't be read by accident."},{"t":"text","title":"Why not flag fields","md":"You could make a struct with fields `paid: bool`, `shipped: bool`, `track: Option<String>`. But then nonsense becomes possible: `shipped = true` while `paid = false`. Or a tracking number on an order that hasn't shipped.\n\nWith an `enum`, such **impossible** states simply can't be written. The motto: *make impossible states unrepresentable*."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why are `enum` states better than a set of `bool` flags?","options":["They take less memory","Impossible combinations can't even be written","`match` is faster than `if`"],"answer":1,"why":["Not the main point, and not always.","Right.","It's not about speed."]},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the door transitions: `Closed` → `open` → `Open`, `Open` → `close` → `Closed`, `Closed` → `lock` → `Locked`. A wrong transition keeps the state and prints `not allowed`.","code":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nenum Door {\n    Open,\n    Closed,\n    Locked,\n}\n\nfn act(d: Door, action: &str) -> Door {\n    match (d, action) {\n        // your code\n        _ => {\n            println!(\"not allowed\");\n            d\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut d = Door::Closed;\n    for a in [\"open\", \"lock\", \"close\", \"lock\"] {\n        d = act(d, a);\n        println!(\"{a} -> {d:?}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nenum Door {\n    Open,\n    Closed,\n    Locked,\n}\n\nfn act(d: Door, action: &str) -> Door {\n    match (d, action) {\n        (Door::Closed, \"open\") => Door::Open,\n        (Door::Open, \"close\") => Door::Closed,\n        (Door::Closed, \"lock\") => Door::Locked,\n        _ => {\n            println!(\"not allowed\");\n            d\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut d = Door::Closed;\n    for a in [\"open\", \"lock\", \"close\", \"lock\"] {\n        d = act(d, a);\n        println!(\"{a} -> {d:?}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"open -> Open\nnot allowed\nlock -> Open\nclose -> Closed\nlock -> Locked","hints":["`match` on a pair: `(Door::Closed, \"open\") => Door::Open,` and so on."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A state is an `enum` variant; the state's data lives inside the variant.","A transition is a `match` on the current state.","Impossible states can't be written."]}],"goals":["Describe \"what state an object is in\" with an `enum`","Make transitions with `match`"]},{"id":"a07-4","title":"Typestate: errors at compile time","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Even stricter than an enum","md":"With an `enum`, a wrong transition is caught **while running** (`not allowed`). Can it be caught **at compile time**?\n\nYes: make each state a **separate type**. Each type has only the allowed methods. No method — no mistake.\n\n> **Analogy:** a subway turnstile. Without a ticket it physically won't let you through. No guard needed to shout at you."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Draft {\n    text: String,\n}\nstruct Review {\n    text: String,\n}\nstruct Published {\n    text: String,\n}\n\nimpl Draft {\n    fn new() -> Draft {\n        Draft { text: String::new() }\n    }\n    fn write(mut self, s: &str) -> Draft {\n        self.text.push_str(s);\n        self\n    }\n    fn submit(self) -> Review {\n        Review { text: self.text }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Review {\n    fn approve(self) -> Published {\n        Published { text: self.text }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Published {\n    fn show(&self) -> &str {\n        &self.text\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let post = Draft::new().write(\"Hello, Rust!\").submit().approve();\n    println!(\"{}\", post.show());\n    // Draft::new().approve(); // error: Draft has no approve\n}","out":"Hello, Rust!","after":"Publishing a draft **without** review is impossible: `Draft` simply has no `approve` method. And `submit(self)` takes the draft — you can't use the old one anymore."},{"t":"text","title":"Where it's useful","md":"- A database connection: you can't send a query until you've called `connect()`.\n- A builder where a required field must be set before `build()`.\n- Protocols: handshake first, data after.\n\nThe cost: more types and more code. Use it when an ordering mistake is **expensive**."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens when you call `Draft::new().approve()` in the example above?","options":["A panic at run time","A compile error: `Draft` has no `approve` method","The post is published without review"],"answer":1,"why":["Worse for the user, and not what happens here.","Right.","That's exactly what typestate prevents."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Typestate with PhantomData","md":"When there are many states but the data is the same, the state goes into a **type parameter**:\n\n```\nuse std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct Locked;\nstruct Unlocked;\n\nstruct Safe<State> {\n    gold: u32,\n    _state: PhantomData<State>,\n}\n\nimpl Safe<Locked> {\n    fn unlock(self) -> Safe<Unlocked> { Safe { gold: self.gold, _state: PhantomData } }\n}\nimpl Safe<Unlocked> {\n    fn take(&self) -> u32 { self.gold }\n}\n```\n`PhantomData<State>` takes no memory. It only \"marks\" the type: `Safe<Locked>` and `Safe<Unlocked>` are different types with different methods."},{"t":"task","md":"Make a typestate for a kettle: `Kettle` (empty) → `fill()` → `FullKettle` → `boil()` → prints `boiling: N ml`. An empty kettle must not have `boil`.","code":"struct Kettle;\nstruct FullKettle {\n    ml: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Kettle {\n    // your code\n}\n\nimpl FullKettle {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Kettle.fill(500).boil();\n}","solution":"struct Kettle;\nstruct FullKettle {\n    ml: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Kettle {\n    fn fill(self, ml: u32) -> FullKettle {\n        FullKettle { ml }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl FullKettle {\n    fn boil(self) {\n        println!(\"boiling: {} ml\", self.ml);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    Kettle.fill(500).boil();\n}","expect":"boiling: 500 ml","hints":["`fill(self, ml: u32) -> FullKettle` in `impl Kettle`, `boil(self)` in `impl FullKettle`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Typestate: each state is its own type with its own methods.","A wrong order doesn't compile.","Transition methods take `self` so the old state disappears."]}],"goals":["Make a wrong call order fail to compile","Understand the idea of \"state in the type\""]},{"id":"a07-p","title":"Practice: patterns","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"A `Username` newtype is created with `parse`: 3 to 12 characters, only Latin letters and digits. Otherwise `Err` with the reason.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Username(String);\n\nimpl Username {\n    fn parse(s: &str) -> Result<Username, String> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"rustacean\", \"ab\", \"кот123\", \"user_1\"] {\n        println!(\"{:?}\", Username::parse(s));\n    }\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Username(String);\n\nimpl Username {\n    fn parse(s: &str) -> Result<Username, String> {\n        let n = s.chars().count();\n        if n < 3 || n > 12 {\n            return Err(String::from(\"length 3 to 12\"));\n        }\n        if !s.chars().all(|c| c.is_ascii_alphanumeric()) {\n            return Err(String::from(\"Latin letters and digits only\"));\n        }\n        Ok(Username(s.to_string()))\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"rustacean\", \"ab\", \"кот123\", \"user_1\"] {\n        println!(\"{:?}\", Username::parse(s));\n    }\n}","expect":"Ok(Username(\"rustacean\"))\nErr(\"length 3 to 12\")\nErr(\"Latin letters and digits only\")\nErr(\"Latin letters and digits only\")","hints":["Count the length in characters with `s.chars().count()`.","`c.is_ascii_alphanumeric()` — a Latin letter or a digit."]},{"t":"task","md":"A player: `Stopped`, `Playing { track }`, `Paused { track }`. The method `press(self, button)`: `\"play\"` from `Stopped` starts the track `\"intro\"`, from `Paused` it resumes; `\"pause\"` from `Playing` pauses; `\"stop\"` from anything gives `Stopped`. Everything else changes nothing.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Player {\n    Stopped,\n    Playing { track: String },\n    Paused { track: String },\n}\n\nimpl Player {\n    fn press(self, button: &str) -> Player {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut p = Player::Stopped;\n    for b in [\"play\", \"pause\", \"pause\", \"play\", \"stop\"] {\n        p = p.press(b);\n        println!(\"{b}: {p:?}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Player {\n    Stopped,\n    Playing { track: String },\n    Paused { track: String },\n}\n\nimpl Player {\n    fn press(self, button: &str) -> Player {\n        match (self, button) {\n            (_, \"stop\") => Player::Stopped,\n            (Player::Stopped, \"play\") => Player::Playing { track: String::from(\"intro\") },\n            (Player::Paused { track }, \"play\") => Player::Playing { track },\n            (Player::Playing { track }, \"pause\") => Player::Paused { track },\n            (other, _) => other,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut p = Player::Stopped;\n    for b in [\"play\", \"pause\", \"pause\", \"play\", \"stop\"] {\n        p = p.press(b);\n        println!(\"{b}: {p:?}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"play: Playing { track: \"intro\" }\npause: Paused { track: \"intro\" }\npause: Paused { track: \"intro\" }\nplay: Playing { track: \"intro\" }\nstop: Stopped","hints":["`match (self, button)` — match on the pair. The last branch is `(other, _) => other`.","The track moves from one variant to the other: `(Player::Paused { track }, \"play\") => Player::Playing { track }`."]},{"t":"task","md":"An email builder: `to` is required, `subject` defaults to `\"(no subject)\"`. The `build` method returns a `Result`: without a recipient — `Err(\"no recipient\")`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Email {\n    to: String,\n    subject: String,\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct EmailBuilder {\n    to: Option<String>,\n    subject: Option<String>,\n}\n\nimpl EmailBuilder {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", EmailBuilder::default().to(\"ada@rust.dev\").build());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", EmailBuilder::default().subject(\"Hi\").build());\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Email {\n    to: String,\n    subject: String,\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct EmailBuilder {\n    to: Option<String>,\n    subject: Option<String>,\n}\n\nimpl EmailBuilder {\n    fn to(mut self, addr: &str) -> Self {\n        self.to = Some(addr.to_string());\n        self\n    }\n    fn subject(mut self, s: &str) -> Self {\n        self.subject = Some(s.to_string());\n        self\n    }\n    fn build(self) -> Result<Email, String> {\n        let to = self.to.ok_or(String::from(\"no recipient\"))?;\n        let subject = self.subject.unwrap_or(String::from(\"(no subject)\"));\n        Ok(Email { to, subject })\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", EmailBuilder::default().to(\"ada@rust.dev\").build());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", EmailBuilder::default().subject(\"Hi\").build());\n}","expect":"Ok(Email { to: \"ada@rust.dev\", subject: \"(no subject)\" })\nErr(\"no recipient\")","hints":["The `to` and `subject` methods put `Some(...)` into the field and return `self`.","In `build`: `self.to.ok_or(...)?` and `self.subject.unwrap_or(...)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A newtype with a check — the data is always valid.","`match (state, action)` — a compact state machine.","A builder can return a `Result` if a required field isn't set."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use newtype, builder and a state machine together"]}]},{"id":"a08","title":"Threads, deeper","sub":"Send and Sync, scope, RwLock, atomics and rayon","lessons":[{"id":"a08-1","title":"Send and Sync","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Two marker traits","md":"Rust has two traits without methods. The compiler gives them to types **by itself**:\n- `Send` — a value can be **sent** to another thread (ownership handed over).\n- `Sync` — a value can be **looked at** from several threads at once (sharing `&T`).\n\nAlmost everything is `Send` and `Sync`: numbers, `String`, `Vec`. The exceptions are types that are unsafe to use at the same time.\n\n> **Analogy:** `Send` — the thing can be mailed as a parcel. `Sync` — the thing can be pinned on a shared board, and everyone can look at it at once."},{"t":"text","title":"Why Rc isn't Send","md":"`Rc` counts owners with a **plain** number. If two threads `clone` at the same moment, the count can go wrong: both read 1, both write 2, when it should be 3. Later the memory gets freed too early.\n\nSo `Rc` isn't `Send`, and the compiler won't let you send it to a thread. Use `Arc` (atomic Rc) instead: it counts owners **atomically**, without glitches. A bit slower, but safe."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let shared = Arc::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);\n    let mut handles = vec![];\n    for i in 0..3 {\n        let data = Arc::clone(&shared);\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || data[i] * 10));\n    }\n    let results: Vec<i32> = handles.into_iter().map(|h| h.join().unwrap()).collect();\n    println!(\"{results:?}\");\n}","out":"[10, 20, 30]","after":"Replace `Arc` with `std::rc::Rc` and run it. The error will say: `Rc<Vec<i32>>` cannot be sent between threads safely."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can't an `Rc` be sent to another thread?","options":["Because it stores data on the heap","Its owner count can go wrong under simultaneous changes","Because it's slow"],"answer":1,"why":["`Arc` does too, and it can be sent.","Right. `Arc` counts atomically.","The opposite: `Rc` is faster than `Arc`."]},{"t":"text","title":"More \"not for threads\" and \"for threads\" pairs","md":"- `Rc` → `Arc`\n- `RefCell` → `Mutex` or `RwLock`\n- `Cell` → atomics (`AtomicUsize`, `AtomicBool`…)\n\nThe rule: if a type is \"single-threaded\", it has a multi-threaded sibling."},{"t":"deep","title":"How the compiler assigns Send and Sync","md":"`Send` and `Sync` are **auto traits**. A struct gets them by itself if **all its fields** have them. One `Rc` field inside is enough — and the whole struct is no longer `Send`.\n\nThere's a precise rule: `T` is `Sync` if `&T` is `Send`. That is, \"sharing a reference is safe\" = \"the reference can be sent to another thread\".\n\nYou can implement these traits by hand only with `unsafe impl Send for T {}`. That's a promise to the compiler that you checked everything yourself. It's rarely needed — mostly by authors of low-level libraries."},{"t":"recap","items":["`Send` — can be handed to a thread; `Sync` — a reference can be shared between threads.","The compiler assigns them based on the fields.","`Rc`/`RefCell` are single-threaded; `Arc`/`Mutex` are their multi-threaded siblings."]}],"goals":["See who decides what can be handed to a thread","Know why `Rc` can't go and `Arc` can"]},{"id":"a08-2","title":"thread::scope: threads that borrow","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The problem with spawn","md":"`thread::spawn` requires `'static`: the thread may outlive the function. So you have to move everything or wrap it in an `Arc`. Even if the thread lives a couple of milliseconds.\n\n`thread::scope` fixes that: all threads inside the \"scope\" **must** finish before you leave it. So they can freely borrow local variables.\n\n> **Analogy:** a kids' party at home. Every guest leaves before you lock the door. So you can lend them your toys — they'll definitely come back."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    let (left, right) = numbers.split_at(3);\n\n    let (a, b) = thread::scope(|s| {\n        let h1 = s.spawn(|| left.iter().sum::<i32>());\n        let h2 = s.spawn(|| right.iter().sum::<i32>());\n        (h1.join().unwrap(), h2.join().unwrap())\n    });\n\n    println!(\"{a} + {b} = {}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"the vector is still ours: {}\", numbers.len());\n}","out":"6 + 15 = 21\nthe vector is still ours: 6","after":"No `Arc`, no `move`, no `clone`. The threads just look at the halves of the vector."},{"t":"text","title":"You can change things too","md":"Inside a `scope` a thread can even take a `&mut` — as long as it's the only one:\n\n```\nlet mut counts = [0; 2];\nthread::scope(|s| {\n    let (a, b) = counts.split_at_mut(1);\n    s.spawn(|| a[0] += 1);\n    s.spawn(|| b[0] += 2);\n});\n// counts == [1, 2]\n```\n`split_at_mut` splits the array into two **non-overlapping** parts. Each thread changes its own."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can threads inside `thread::scope` take references to local variables?","options":["All the scope's threads must finish before it's left","Because the checks are turned off there","Because the data is copied into each thread"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. The references won't outlive the data.","The checks work as usual.","Nothing is copied."]},{"t":"task","md":"Count in two threads inside `thread::scope`: the first counts even numbers, the second counts odd ones. Print `even 3, odd 4`.","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7];\n    let (even, odd) = thread::scope(|s| {\n        let e = s.spawn(|| v.iter().filter(|x| *x % 2 == 0).count());\n        let o = s.spawn(|| v.iter().filter(|x| *x % 2 == 1).count());\n        (e.join().unwrap(), o.join().unwrap())\n    });\n    println!(\"even {even}, odd {odd}\");\n}","expect":"even 3, odd 4","hints":["`thread::scope(|s| { ... })` — inside it `s.spawn(|| ...)`. The scope's result is whatever the block returns.","Return a pair: `(e.join().unwrap(), o.join().unwrap())`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`thread::scope` — threads finish before the scope is left.","So they can get `&` and even `&mut` to local data.","No `Arc` and no `move` needed."]}],"goals":["Start threads that read local data without `Arc` and `move`","See why it's safe"]},{"id":"a08-3","title":"RwLock and atomics","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"RwLock: many readers or one writer","md":"A `Mutex` lets **one** in at a time — whether it reads or writes. If reads are frequent and writes are rare, that's a needless queue.\n\nAn `RwLock` (read-write lock) lets in:\n- **any number** of readers at once — `read()`;\n- or **one** writer — `write()`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a museum. Many people can look at the paintings at once. But when a restorer works on a painting, the hall is closed to everyone."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let config = Arc::new(RwLock::new(String::from(\"dark theme\")));\n\n    let readers: Vec<_> = (0..3).map(|_| {\n        let c = Arc::clone(&config);\n        thread::spawn(move || c.read().unwrap().len()) // they read at the same time\n    }).collect();\n    for r in readers {\n        r.join().unwrap();\n    }\n\n    {\n        let mut w = config.write().unwrap();\n        *w = String::from(\"light theme\");\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", config.read().unwrap());\n}","out":"light theme"},{"t":"text","title":"Atomics: no lock at all","md":"For simple things — a counter, a flag — you don't need a lock. There are **atomic** types: `AtomicUsize`, `AtomicBool`, `AtomicI64`… The processor performs their operations \"in one indivisible step\".\n\n```\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nlet hits = AtomicUsize::new(0);\nhits.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed); // +1, safe from any thread\nlet n = hits.load(Ordering::Relaxed);\n```\n`Ordering::Relaxed` is enough for simple counters. What it means is in the deep dive."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let hits = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n    thread::scope(|s| {\n        for _ in 0..4 {\n            s.spawn(|| {\n                for _ in 0..1000 {\n                    hits.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n                }\n            });\n        }\n    });\n    println!(\"{}\", hits.load(Ordering::Relaxed));\n}","out":"4000","after":"Four threads, a thousand times each — exactly 4000, no lock. Note: an atomic is changed through `&`, no `mut` needed."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Data is read often from many threads and changed rarely. What's better?","options":["`Mutex`","`RwLock`","`RefCell`"],"answer":1,"why":["It works, but readers will wait for each other for nothing.","Right. Readers don't get in each other's way.","It's not for threads."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"You need a shared request counter for all threads. What's simplest?","options":["`AtomicUsize`","`Arc<Rc<usize>>`","A plain `usize` in a `static mut`"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. No lock, no `mut`.","`Rc` can't go to threads, and you can't change through it.","Unsafe and requires `unsafe`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"What Ordering is","md":"The processor and the compiler **reorder** operations for speed when it's invisible within one thread. But another thread may see the changes in a different order than you wrote them.\n\n`Ordering` says which order must be preserved:\n- `Relaxed` — only the operation itself is indivisible; no order guarantee relative to others. Enough for counters.\n- `Release` (on write) + `Acquire` (on read) — \"everything I did before the write will be visible to whoever read it\". That's how \"data ready\" flags are built.\n- `SeqCst` — the strictest: all threads see one common order. Slower, but easier to reason about.\n\nNot sure? Use `SeqCst`. Want to go deeper? Mara Bos's book \"Rust Atomics and Locks\" (free online)."},{"t":"recap","items":["`RwLock`: many `read()`s at once or one `write()`.","Atomics (`AtomicUsize`…) — counters and flags without a lock.","For a simple counter — `fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed)`."]}],"goals":["Choose between `Mutex` and `RwLock`","Count things across threads without a lock — with atomics"]},{"id":"a08-4","title":"rayon: parallel with one word","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A parallel iterator","md":"The **rayon** crate takes an ordinary iterator chain and spreads the work over all CPU cores. Most of the time the change is one word:\n\n```\nuse rayon::prelude::*;\n\nlet sum: u64 = data.iter().map(heavy).sum();     // one core\nlet sum: u64 = data.par_iter().map(heavy).sum(); // all cores\n```\nrayon splits the data into pieces, creates the threads and gathers the result by itself.\n\n> **Analogy:** peeling a sack of potatoes alone, or calling the whole family. The sack is split into piles, and everyone peels their own."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use rayon::prelude::*;\n\nfn is_prime(n: u64) -> bool {\n    n >= 2 && (2..).take_while(|d| d * d <= n).all(|d| n % d != 0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let count = (1..200_000u64).into_par_iter().filter(|&n| is_prime(n)).count();\n    println!(\"primes: {count}\");\n\n    let words = vec![\"rust\", \"crab\", \"ferris\"];\n    let lens: Vec<usize> = words.par_iter().map(|w| w.chars().count()).collect();\n    println!(\"{lens:?}\");\n}","out":"primes: 17984\n[4, 4, 6]","after":"`collect` after `par_iter` keeps the **original order**, even though the work ran in parallel."},{"t":"text","title":"When it won't help","md":"- There's little work (adding 100 numbers). Handing out the work takes longer than doing it.\n- Each step waits for the network or disk. That calls for async, not more cores.\n- Steps depend on each other (each one uses the previous one).\n\nThe rule: **measure** first (see the speed module), then parallelize."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You added 10 numbers with `par_iter` and it got slower. Why?","options":["Handing work out to threads costs more than the work itself","rayon is broken","The numbers were added wrong"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. Parallelism pays off on large amounts.","rayon is fine, the task is just too small.","The result is correct, just slower."]},{"t":"recap","items":["rayon: `iter()` → `par_iter()`, `into_iter()` → `into_par_iter()`.","The order in `collect` is kept.","Helps with heavy computation, not with tiny jobs or waiting on the network."]}],"goals":["Parallelize work by changing `iter` to `par_iter`","Know when it speeds things up and when it doesn't"]},{"id":"a08-p","title":"Practice: threads","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Split the vector into 4 pieces with `chunks` and sum each one in its own thread inside `thread::scope`. Add up the results and print them.","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data: Vec<u64> = (1..=100).collect();\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data: Vec<u64> = (1..=100).collect();\n    let total: u64 = thread::scope(|s| {\n        let handles: Vec<_> = data.chunks(25).map(|c| s.spawn(move || c.iter().sum::<u64>())).collect();\n        handles.into_iter().map(|h| h.join().unwrap()).sum()\n    });\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"5050","hints":["`data.chunks(25)` gives 4 pieces. For each one — `s.spawn(move || c.iter().sum::<u64>())`.","`move` here takes only the reference to the piece `c`; the data itself stays in `data`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A stop flag: `AtomicBool`. The worker thread counts to 1000 but checks the flag and stops if it's raised. We raise the flag **before** starting the thread, so the thread won't take a single step.","code":"use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);\n    // raise the flag\n    let done = thread::scope(|s| {\n        s.spawn(|| {\n            let mut n = 0;\n            while n < 1000 {\n                // check the flag\n                n += 1;\n            }\n            n\n        }).join().unwrap()\n    });\n    println!(\"steps done: {done}\");\n}","solution":"use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);\n    stop.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst);\n    let done = thread::scope(|s| {\n        s.spawn(|| {\n            let mut n = 0;\n            while n < 1000 {\n                if stop.load(Ordering::SeqCst) {\n                    break;\n                }\n                n += 1;\n            }\n            n\n        }).join().unwrap()\n    });\n    println!(\"steps done: {done}\");\n}","expect":"steps done: 0","hints":["Raise it: `stop.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst)`. Check it: `if stop.load(Ordering::SeqCst) { break; }`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A shared list `Arc<RwLock<Vec<String>>>`. Three threads each **add** one name with `write()`. Then the main thread uses `read()` to sort a copy and print it.","code":"use std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let names = Arc::new(RwLock::new(Vec::new()));\n    let mut handles = vec![];\n    for n in [\"Vicky\", \"Ann\", \"Bob\"] {\n        // your code\n    }\n    for h in handles {\n        h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    let mut sorted = names.read().unwrap().clone();\n    sorted.sort();\n    println!(\"{sorted:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let names = Arc::new(RwLock::new(Vec::new()));\n    let mut handles = vec![];\n    for n in [\"Vicky\", \"Ann\", \"Bob\"] {\n        let names = Arc::clone(&names);\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {\n            names.write().unwrap().push(n.to_string());\n        }));\n    }\n    for h in handles {\n        h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    let mut sorted = names.read().unwrap().clone();\n    sorted.sort();\n    println!(\"{sorted:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\"]","hints":["On each loop step: clone the `Arc`, start `thread::spawn(move || ...)` and push the thread into `handles`.","Inside the thread: `names.write().unwrap().push(n.to_string());`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`scope` + `chunks` — a simple way to split work.","`AtomicBool` — a stop flag without a lock.","`RwLock::write()` to change, `read()` to read."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Solve tasks with `scope`, atomics and `RwLock`"]}]},{"id":"a09","title":"Async from the inside","sub":"Future, a tiny runtime of your own, tokio, join, spawn, select","lessons":[{"id":"a09-1","title":"What a Future is","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The main idea again","md":"In Level 1 we said: async means \"wait without sitting idle\". While one task waits for the network, another one works.\n\nNow let's look inside. Calling an `async fn` **doesn't run it**. It returns a **Future** — an object meaning \"the result comes later\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a coat-check ticket. You get it right away, and the coat when your turn comes. The ticket won't fetch the coat by itself: you need the attendant."},{"t":"text","title":"How a Future works","md":"The `Future` trait has one main method — `poll`:\n\n```\ntrait Future {\n    type Output;\n    fn poll(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context) -> Poll<Self::Output>;\n}\n```\n`poll` returns:\n- `Poll::Ready(value)` — done;\n- `Poll::Pending` — not yet, call me later.\n\nSomeone has to call `poll`. That someone is called a **runtime** (or executor). The most popular one is `tokio`."},{"t":"code","md":"A tiny runtime of our own — 10 lines. It just polls the Future until it says \"ready\":","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    println!(\"computing\");\n    a + b\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let f = add(2, 3);\n    println!(\"future created\");\n    let r = block_on(f);\n    println!(\"result: {r}\");\n}","out":"future created\ncomputing\nresult: 5","after":"Look at the order: `computing` appeared **after** `future created`. Calling `add(2, 3)` computed nothing — it only created the Future."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens if you call an `async fn` and don't `.await` it (or hand it to a runtime)?","options":["The function runs in the background","Nothing: the function body never starts","A compile error"],"answer":1,"why":["Without polling, a Future does nothing.","Right. Futures are lazy.","It compiles, but the compiler warns that the Future is unused."]},{"t":"text","title":"What .await does","md":"`.await` inside another `async fn` means: \"poll this Future. If it's `Pending`, I'm `Pending` too — give control back to the runtime\".\n\nThat makes a chain: the runtime polls the outer Future, which polls the inner one, and so on all the way down. Somewhere at the bottom there's real waiting: a timer, the network, a file."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn price() -> u32 { 100 }\nasync fn tax(p: u32) -> u32 { p / 5 }\n\nasync fn total() -> u32 {\n    let p = price().await;\n    let t = tax(p).await;\n    p + t\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(total()));\n}","out":"120"},{"t":"deep","title":"Waker: how not to poll for nothing","md":"Our `block_on` polls the Future in an endless loop. If the Future waits a second for the network, the CPU spins idly.\n\nReal runtimes are smarter. The `Context` holds a **Waker** — an \"alarm clock\". A Future that returned `Pending` stores it and calls `wake()` when the data arrives. The runtime polls only the Futures that were \"woken up\".\n\n`Waker::noop()` in our example is an alarm that does nothing. Good enough for a toy runtime: we poll all the time anyway.\n\nAnd `pin!` is needed because a Future made from an `async fn` may hold references **to itself** (to its own local variables). Such an object must not move in memory after the first `poll`. `Pin` is the promise \"I won't move it\". More in the `select` lesson."},{"t":"recap","items":["An `async fn` returns a Future — \"the result comes later\".","A Future is lazy: it works only while it's polled (`poll`).","The runtime does the polling. `.await` passes the polling deeper."]}],"goals":["See that an `async fn` returns a \"promise\", not a result","See that a Future does nothing until it's polled"]},{"id":"a09-2","title":"tokio and join!","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"tokio — a real runtime","md":"`tokio` can do everything our toy `block_on` can't: timers, networking, files, many threads. You add it with one line above `main`:\n\n```\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    // you can write .await here\n}\n```\nThe attribute turns `async fn main` into a normal `main` that starts the runtime."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\nasync fn cook(name: &str, secs: u64) -> String {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(secs * 100)).await;\n    format!(\"{name} is ready\")\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let start = std::time::Instant::now();\n    let a = cook(\"tea\", 3).await;\n    let b = cook(\"toast\", 2).await;\n    println!(\"{a}, {b}\");\n    println!(\"one by one: ~{} ms\", start.elapsed().as_millis() / 100 * 100);\n}","out":"tea is ready, toast is ready\none by one: ~500 ms","after":"`.await` one after another — **in turn**: 300 + 200 = 500 ms."},{"t":"text","title":"join! — wait for everything at once","md":"`tokio::join!(a, b, c)` runs several Futures **at the same time** and waits until all of them finish. It returns a tuple of results.\n\n> **Analogy:** turning on the kettle and the toaster at the same time, instead of waiting for the kettle before switching on the toaster."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\nasync fn cook(name: &str, secs: u64) -> String {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(secs * 100)).await;\n    format!(\"{name} is ready\")\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let start = std::time::Instant::now();\n    let (a, b) = tokio::join!(cook(\"tea\", 3), cook(\"toast\", 2));\n    println!(\"{a}, {b}\");\n    println!(\"together: ~{} ms\", start.elapsed().as_millis() / 100 * 100);\n}","out":"tea is ready, toast is ready\ntogether: ~300 ms","after":"300 ms, not 500: while we waited for the tea, the toast was toasting too."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Three requests of 1 second each. How long does `join!` take for all three?","options":["3 seconds","About 1 second","0 seconds"],"answer":1,"why":["That's if you `.await` them one by one.","Right. They wait at the same time.","You still have to wait."]},{"t":"text","title":"Important: join! is not threads","md":"`join!` works on **one** thread. Tasks take turns making progress while others wait. So it helps with **waiting** (network, timers), but doesn't speed up heavy computation. For computation — rayon or threads."},{"t":"recap","items":["`#[tokio::main]` — run an async `main` on tokio.","`.await` one by one — the times add up.","`tokio::join!(a, b)` — wait at once; the time is the longest one."]}],"goals":["Run an async program on tokio","Do several waits at once with `join!`"]},{"id":"a09-3","title":"spawn and channels","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"spawn — a separate task","md":"`tokio::spawn(future)` hands the Future to the runtime as an **independent task**. It starts working right away, even if you're not waiting for its result yet. It returns a `JoinHandle` — use it to wait for the result.\n\nSimilar to `thread::spawn`, but tasks are **lightweight**: you can start thousands of them."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let mut handles = Vec::new();\n    for id in 1..=3 {\n        handles.push(tokio::spawn(async move {\n            sleep(Duration::from_millis(30 * (4 - id))).await;\n            println!(\"task {id} finished\");\n            id * 10\n        }));\n    }\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for h in handles {\n        sum += h.await.unwrap();\n    }\n    println!(\"sum: {sum}\");\n}","out":"task 3 finished\ntask 2 finished\ntask 1 finished\nsum: 60","after":"The tasks finished in reverse order (the third has the shortest sleep), but we collected the results in order."},{"t":"text","title":"Why async move","md":"`tokio::spawn` requires `'static`, just like `thread::spawn`: the task may outlive the function. So you almost always write `async move { ... }` — to take the needed data inside."},{"t":"text","title":"Channels between tasks","md":"tokio has its own channels: `tokio::sync::mpsc`. Like with threads, except sending and receiving use `.await`.\n\n```\nlet (tx, mut rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(16); // 16 is the queue size\ntx.send(value).await.unwrap();\nwhile let Some(v) = rx.recv().await { ... }\n```\nWhen all senders (`tx`) are dropped, `recv()` returns `None` — the loop ends."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use tokio::sync::mpsc;\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel(8);\n    for worker in 1..=3 {\n        let tx = tx.clone();\n        tokio::spawn(async move {\n            tx.send(worker * worker).await.unwrap();\n        });\n    }\n    drop(tx); // the main sender is no longer needed\n\n    let mut got = Vec::new();\n    while let Some(v) = rx.recv().await {\n        got.push(v);\n    }\n    got.sort();\n    println!(\"{got:?}\");\n}","out":"[1, 4, 9]","after":"Without `drop(tx)` the loop would wait forever: as long as any sender is alive, the channel stays open."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why write `drop(tx)` after the `for` loop with `tx.clone()`?","options":["So the channel closes once the tasks have sent their values, and `recv()` returns `None`","To free memory","Otherwise the tasks won't start"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","Not the main point.","They will, but the receive loop won't end."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`tokio::spawn(async move { ... })` — an independent lightweight task.","`handle.await` — wait for its result.","`mpsc::channel` + `send().await` / `recv().await`. All `tx` dropped → `None`."]}],"goals":["Start independent tasks with `tokio::spawn`","Pass data between tasks through a channel"]},{"id":"a09-4","title":"select! and timeouts","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"select! — who's first","md":"`tokio::select!` waits on **several** Futures and takes the result of the **first** one to finish. The rest are cancelled.\n\n```\ntokio::select! {\n    v = task_a => println!(\"A was first: {v}\"),\n    v = task_b => println!(\"B was first: {v}\"),\n}\n```\n> **Analogy:** waiting for a bus at two stops at once. Whichever bus comes first is the one you take."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\nasync fn server(name: &str, ms: u64) -> String {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(ms)).await;\n    format!(\"reply from {name}\")\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    tokio::select! {\n        r = server(\"Europe\", 120) => println!(\"{r}\"),\n        r = server(\"Asia\", 40) => println!(\"{r}\"),\n    }\n}","out":"reply from Asia","after":"We didn't even wait for the slow server: its Future was simply dropped."},{"t":"text","title":"timeout — limit the wait","md":"A common `select!` case is \"the result, or time's up\". There's a ready-made function for it:\n\n```\nuse tokio::time::{timeout, Duration};\n\nmatch timeout(Duration::from_secs(2), request()).await {\n    Ok(reply) => ...,\n    Err(_) => println!(\"too slow\"),\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::{sleep, timeout};\n\nasync fn slow_answer() -> u32 {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(500)).await;\n    42\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    match timeout(Duration::from_millis(100), slow_answer()).await {\n        Ok(v) => println!(\"answer: {v}\"),\n        Err(_) => println!(\"time's up\"),\n    }\n    match timeout(Duration::from_millis(900), slow_answer()).await {\n        Ok(v) => println!(\"answer: {v}\"),\n        Err(_) => println!(\"time's up\"),\n    }\n}","out":"time's up\nanswer: 42"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens to the other `select!` branches when one finishes?","options":["They keep working in the background","They are cancelled","They wait their turn"],"answer":1,"why":["No, their Futures are dropped.","Right. That's why cancelling midway must not corrupt data.","`select!` takes only one result."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Cancellation and Pin","md":"In async Rust, cancelling a task = **stop polling it and drop** the Future. Cancellation can happen at any `.await`. So write code that keeps data consistent between two `.await`s. For example, don't hold a \"half-written\" file while waiting.\n\nAbout `Pin`: a Future from an `async fn` stores its local variables inside itself. If one variable is a reference to another and the Future is moved in memory, the reference would point to nothing. `Pin<&mut F>` guarantees \"I won't move it anymore\". `tokio::pin!` or `std::pin::pin!` fix a Future in place when it must be polled several times (for example, in a `select!` inside a loop)."},{"t":"recap","items":["`select!` — the first to finish wins, the rest are cancelled.","`timeout(duration, future)` → `Ok(result)` or `Err(time's up)`.","Cancellation can happen at any `.await`."]}],"goals":["Wait for \"whatever happens first\" with `select!`","Put a time limit on waiting with `timeout`"]},{"id":"a09-p","title":"Practice: async","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Use the ready-made `block_on`. Write `async fn double(x)` and `async fn sum_doubled(v)`, which doubles each number with `.await` and adds them up.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(sum_doubled(vec![1, 2, 3])));\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn double(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    x * 2\n}\n\nasync fn sum_doubled(v: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for x in v {\n        total += double(x).await;\n    }\n    total\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(sum_doubled(vec![1, 2, 3])));\n}","expect":"12","hints":["`async fn double(x: i32) -> i32 { x * 2 }`.","In `sum_doubled`, inside the loop: `total += double(x).await;`."]},{"t":"task","crates":true,"md":"With tokio: start three downloads at once with `join!` and print their results on one line separated by commas.","code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\nasync fn fetch(name: &str, ms: u64) -> String {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(ms)).await;\n    name.to_uppercase()\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\nasync fn fetch(name: &str, ms: u64) -> String {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(ms)).await;\n    name.to_uppercase()\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let (a, b, c) = tokio::join!(fetch(\"cat\", 30), fetch(\"dog\", 10), fetch(\"fox\", 20));\n    println!(\"{a}, {b}, {c}\");\n}","expect":"CAT, DOG, FOX","hints":["`let (a, b, c) = tokio::join!(fetch(\"cat\", 30), fetch(\"dog\", 10), fetch(\"fox\", 20));`."]},{"t":"task","crates":true,"md":"With tokio: \"a quick answer or a fallback\". If `slow()` doesn't finish within 50 ms, print `fallback answer`. Use `timeout`.","code":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::{sleep, timeout};\n\nasync fn slow() -> &'static str {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(300)).await;\n    \"the real answer\"\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::{sleep, timeout};\n\nasync fn slow() -> &'static str {\n    sleep(Duration::from_millis(300)).await;\n    \"the real answer\"\n}\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    let answer = timeout(Duration::from_millis(50), slow()).await.unwrap_or(\"fallback answer\");\n    println!(\"{answer}\");\n}","expect":"fallback answer","hints":["`timeout(...)` returns a `Result`. `unwrap_or(\"fallback answer\")` gives the fallback on error."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`async fn` + `.await` work even in a home-made runtime.","`join!` — everything at once, `timeout` — with a time limit."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write async code with your own tiny runtime and with tokio"]}]}],"glossary":[]};
